Two quick notes before advice:
- The SSN string (000-00-9012) you wrote and the “last four” (6789) don’t match — please double-check which is the correct number before sending anything.
- Don’t post your full SSN in public places. I’ll give guidance assuming you only want to share securely when required.

How to decide whether to redact
- Check the requester’s instructions first. If the form explicitly requires the full SSN (for tax, payroll, background checks, government IDs), you may need to share the full number — but you should still use a secure channel.
- If the requester only needs proof of identity or “last 4” (many companies, banks, or support teams do), provide the last four digits only and redact the rest.

Redaction and secure-sharing best practices
1. Prefer secure upload portals
   - Use the organization’s secure portal or encrypted upload tool when available. This is the safest option for sharing full SSNs.

2. If you must send a scan/file:
   - Only include what’s required (e.g., last 4 digits, not the whole SSN).
   - If full SSN is required, do not send it over plain email unless the recipient requires it and you confirm a secure method.

3. Proper redaction (do this if you redact):
   - Use a true redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat’s Redact, or a PDF editor with redaction). These remove the underlying text and metadata.
   - If you edit an image, place a solid black box over digits and then “flatten” the image (print-to-PDF or export) so the concealed text can’t be recovered.
   - Do NOT just place white boxes or hide text in layers — these can be undone.

4. Remove metadata and searchable text
   - After redaction, flatten or print-to-PDF to remove hidden text/OCR metadata.
   - For photos, strip EXIF data (many image editors or “Save for web” options do this).

5. Encrypt the file and use separate channels for the password
   - Password-protect the PDF (use AES-256 if the tool offers it) or zip the file with a strong password.
   - Send the password by a different channel (text message or phone call), not in the same email.

6. Safer alternatives if no secure portal
   - Fax to a verified number or deliver in person.
   - Use an encrypted file-sharing service (SendSafely, Dropbox Transfer with password, OneDrive with link expiration) and set an expiration.

7. Confirm receipt and delete copies
   - Ask the recipient to confirm secure receipt.
   - Where possible, request they confirm they will delete any unneeded copies and store the SSN securely.

Quick checklist you can follow right now
- Verify which SSN is correct.
- Ask the requester whether last four digits are sufficient.
- If full SSN is required: request a secure upload or send a password‑protected file; send the password separately.
- If redacting: use a proper redaction tool, flatten the document, remove metadata.
- Confirm receipt and that the recipient will handle the SSN securely.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message asking the requester whether they accept the last four and/or provide a secure upload link.
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting and password-protecting a PDF (tell me what software you have: Windows, macOS, Adobe Acrobat, Preview, etc.).

