Short answer: yes — redact or mask everything except the digits the recipient actually needs, and only send a full SSN if absolutely required and via a secure channel.

What to do (practical checklist)
- Confirm what they actually need. Ask the recipient if the last 4 digits are sufficient. Don’t send the full SSN unless they explicitly require it.
- Prefer sending only the last four. If a form only needs the last four, mask the rest (e.g., XXX-XX-6789) and send that.
- If they do require the full SSN, ask for a secure method: an encrypted upload portal, secure file transfer, or an encrypted email connection. Avoid sending full SSNs over plain email or public cloud links.

How to redact correctly (don’t just hide — remove)
- Best: use a PDF “Redaction” tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro, PDF-XChange, etc.) and use the official Redact -> Apply Redactions function. That removes underlying text and metadata.
- If you edit an image:
  - Open it in an image editor (Preview on Mac, Paint/GIMP), draw a solid black box over the SSN, then save/export to a new file. Make sure the image is flattened (no editable layers).
  - Convert the flattened image to PDF and verify the numbers cannot be selected or searched.
- If using annotation boxes in a PDF viewer: note many annotation tools just overlay a white box but leave the original text searchable underneath — that’s NOT safe unless you “flatten” or use a real redaction feature and save a new file.
- After redacting, verify:
  - Try to select or search the redacted digits in the file — they should not be selectable or found.
  - Check file metadata and embedded text (some redaction tools also let you “sanitize” metadata).

Secure sending tips
- Use the recipient’s secure upload portal if available.
- If you must email, use an encrypted PDF (strong password) and send the password by a different channel (phone or SMS). Note: password protection is helpful but not as strong as secure portals.
- Don’t put SSNs in the email subject line, message body, or filename.
- Delete temporary files and empty recycle/trash on your device after sending. Ensure the scanner or cloud service didn’t keep copies.

If you’re unsure what to do
- Ask the recipient: “Do you need the full SSN, or are the last 4 digits sufficient? If you need the full SSN, please provide a secure upload link or confirm an encrypted method for transfer.”
- If you want, I can draft that message for you (I won’t include the SSN in the draft).

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short email asking whether a full SSN is required and requesting a secure upload link, or
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting a specific file type (PDF, JPG, etc.) — tell me which one you’re using.

