Short answer: yes — treat the full SSN as highly sensitive. If the recipient only needs the last four digits, redact (remove/black out) the first five. If the form truly requires the full SSN, do not send an unprotected scan by regular email — use a secure method.

What to do (step-by-step)

1. Confirm what's required
- Ask the recipient whether they really need the full SSN or whether the last four digits will do. Ask for their preferred secure upload method.

2. If only the last four are needed
- Redact the first five digits so only the last four are visible. Example (do not use a real number in shared text): XXX-XX-1234.
- Use a permanent redaction method (see below), not just covering with white/black shapes that leave the underlying text intact.

3. If the full SSN is required
- Use a secure channel: a trusted secure upload portal, an encrypted email service, a secure file transfer (SFTP), or deliver in person/over the phone.
- If you must email, send a properly encrypted or password-protected file (see options below) and send the password separately (phone or SMS).

How to redact safely
- Use a true redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact) which removes underlying text and metadata.
- If you don't have a redaction tool:
  - Print the form, black out the digits with a permanent marker, then scan the paper. Verify the scanned image no longer contains the numbers (try copying/selecting text).
  - Or open the scanned image in an image editor and permanently paint over the digits, then save/export as a flattened image/PDF.
- Do NOT merely place a black rectangle in a PDF viewer that leaves underlying text selectable; that can be uncovered.
- After redaction, open the file and try selecting/copying text to ensure the digits are unrecoverable.

Other important steps
- Remove metadata/EXIF from photos (phone photos can keep location and other info).
- Avoid putting SSN (full or last four) in the email subject, body, or file name.
- Limit other exposed PII in the scan (DOB, driver’s license number, etc.) unless required.
- If sending a password-protected file: use strong password and modern encryption (PDF with AES-256, or encrypted ZIP). Send the password by a separate channel (phone/SMS).
- Keep a copy of the redacted version (not the unredacted) for your records. Destroy/shred printed copies of unneeded originals.

Quick checklist before you send
- Is full SSN actually required? If not, redact to last four.
- Was redaction done with a tool that removes underlying text? Yes/No.
- Is the file encrypted or uploaded via a secure portal? Yes/No.
- Did you avoid putting numbers in subject/body/file name? Yes/No.
- Did you send the password separately if used? Yes/No.

If you want, tell me:
- Does the recipient accept a secure upload or a passworded PDF? I can give exact steps for your phone/computer (Windows, macOS, iPhone, Android) and recommend tools.

