I won’t repeat your full SSN here, but I can help you decide what to redact and how to send a scan safely.

Key points
- Only share the full SSN when absolutely required. Many forms accept (or only request) the last 4 digits — if the form only asks for the last four, redact the rest before sending.
- Avoid sending a full SSN by plain email or insecure file-sharing. Prefer a secure portal, encrypted email, or phone transmission if possible.
- Simple visual “black boxes” can be reversible if the file still contains underlying text or metadata. Use proper redaction tools and verify the redaction actually removed the text.

How to redact a scanned document (practical steps)
1. Use a proper redaction tool (recommended)
   - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply. Then go to Tools → Protect → Remove Hidden Information (or “Sanitize”) to strip metadata and hidden text. Save as a new file.
2. If you only have an image (photo or JPEG)
   - In an image editor (or Preview/Paint), draw a solid black rectangle over the SSN, then export/save as a new flattened image (PNG/JPG). Don’t rely on layers — flatten first.
   - After saving, open the file and try selecting/searching text. If the SSN can still be selected/copied, the redaction is NOT secure.
3. Free/low-cost alternatives
   - PDF editors like PDF-XChange Editor, PDFescape, or free “redact” utilities can work — but verify they perform true redaction (remove underlying text).
   - Avoid unknown online redaction services for highly sensitive documents unless you trust their security and privacy policies.
4. Verify redaction worked
   - Try to copy/paste or search for the SSN in the redacted file. If you can find the numbers, redo redaction with a different method.
   - Inspect/remove metadata (file properties) and hidden content before sending.

How to send the redacted file safely
- Best: upload via the organization’s secure portal (HTTPS) or a secure document submission system.
- If you must email:
  - Password-protect the PDF with strong encryption (AES-256) and send the password over a different channel (text or phone).
  - Or use encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) if you and the recipient support it.
- Alternatively, ask the recipient if they will accept the last four digits only, or if they prefer you to give the full number by phone/in person.

Other safety steps
- Confirm the recipient’s identity and that they actually need the SSN.
- Keep a minimal copy: delete local copies when no longer needed and securely wipe or shred physical documents.
- If the recipient requires full SSN, ask them to provide a secure upload link or an alternative secure method.

If you tell me which platform or app you’re using to create/send the scan (Windows, macOS, phone app, Adobe Reader, etc.), I can give step‑by‑step redaction instructions for that tool.

