Assistant: I can help — and I won’t repeat your SSN here. Short answer: don’t send the full SSN in an ordinary email or scan unless the recipient explicitly requires it and provides a secure way to receive it. In most cases sharing only the last four digits is sufficient. If the full SSN is required, use a secure upload method or strong file-level protection and verify the recipient.

What to do (priority order)
- Best: Use the requester’s secure portal/file upload. This is the safest way to transmit full SSNs.
- If portal not available: send a password‑protected PDF (AES encryption) and give the password by a separate channel (phone/SMS).
- If you must email: use encrypted email (S/MIME/PGP) or a secure file-transfer service. Verify the exact recipient address before sending.
- If you don’t need to send the full SSN: redact everything except the last four digits.

How to redact safely
- Digital PDFs (recommended if you have Adobe Acrobat Pro or similar):
  1. Use the official Redact tool (not just drawing a black box).
  2. Apply redactions and use the “Sanitize Document” or “Remove Hidden Information” option.
  3. Save as a new file and verify that the removed numbers cannot be selected/copied.
- If you don’t have a proper redaction tool:
  - Print the document, permanently black out the SSN with a thick permanent marker, then photocopy/scan the redacted copy and send the scan. (Do not send the original with ink lightly covering it — marker + photocopy is safer.)
  - Alternatively, cover the SSN on the paper with an opaque sticker or cut out that portion, then scan the copy.
- Avoid: placing a black box over text in a PDF without flattening/sanitizing (this often leaves the underlying text searchable/recoverable).

Quick verification checklist before sending
- Confirm the recipient and that they actually need the full SSN (ask whether last 4 digits are sufficient).
- Open the final file and try to select or copy the redacted digits — if you can, the redaction is not secure.
- Remove file metadata (document properties) if possible.
- Use a new filename that doesn’t include SSN or sensitive words.
- Send password separately (never in the same email).

If the organization is requesting a scanned form, ask them:
- “Do you have a secure upload portal or preferred secure method to receive SSNs?”
- “Is the last four digits sufficient, or do you need the full SSN?”

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short message you can send to the requester asking whether a secure upload is available or confirming that just the last four are acceptable?

