Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — unless the recipient explicitly needs your full SSN, you should redact everything except the last four digits before sending a scan. And if the full SSN is required, send it only by a secure method (secure portal, encrypted email, or in person).

What to do and why
- Only share the last 4 digits when that’s all the form asks for. The first five digits are sensitive and should be removed.
- If the recipient requires the full SSN, use a secure channel (official portal, in-person, or encrypted transfer). Avoid plain email whenever possible.

Secure redaction — recommended methods
1. Use a proper redaction tool (best)
   - PDF: Adobe Acrobat Pro (Tools → Redact → Mark Text for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Save a new file). This permanently removes the text and any hidden content.
   - Other PDF tools with a “redact” feature (Foxit, Nitro, PDFpen) work similarly.
2. If you don’t have a redaction tool, create a new file that contains only the allowed info
   - Copy the required fields (or take a photo of only the area with the last four) into a fresh document or image, save as PDF, and send that. Don’t simply cover text in the original file and send it — underlying text may remain.
3. For image scans/photos
   - Edit the image in an editor (Paint, Preview, Photoshop) to permanently erase the first five digits, then export a new image. After editing, remove image metadata (see below).
4. Scanning tip
   - If scanning paper, physically cover the first five digits with opaque tape or a post-it, then scan the page. Save the scanned file as a new file.

Things to avoid
- Don’t just place a white box (annotation) over the SSN in a PDF or Word doc and send the file without flattening/exporting — the text can often be recovered.
- Don’t send full SSN over plain, unencrypted email unless absolutely necessary.
- Don’t include the SSN in the filename or in metadata.

Check that redaction succeeded
- Try to search the PDF/image for the redacted digits — they should not be findable.
- Open the file in different viewers or copy text to a text editor to verify the full SSN is gone.
- For images, confirm OCR (if used) doesn’t extract the removed numbers.
- Remove metadata (PDF properties / image EXIF) before sending.

Secure delivery options
- Official secure upload portal from the organization (best).
- Encrypted email (S/MIME, PGP) or password-protected PDF with the password sent separately by phone/text.
- In-person delivery or secure fax if accepted.

One more note about what you posted here
- I noticed the SSN you typed (000-00-9012) and the “last four: 6789” don’t match. For your safety, avoid posting actual SSNs in chat. If you need hands-on help with a file, tell me what method or software you have and I can give step-by-step redaction instructions without you re-sending the SSN.

If you tell me:
- whether the form only needs the last 4 or the full SSN, and
- what software you have (Adobe Acrobat, Preview on Mac, Windows, mobile phone),
I can give concise, step-by-step redaction instructions for that tool.

