Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: you should avoid sending the full SSN in an ordinary (unencrypted) email or scan unless the recipient explicitly requires it and provides a secure upload method. In most cases it’s safer to send only the last four digits (you mentioned the last four are 6789) or to use a secure transfer method when the full SSN is required.

Practical guidance and checklist

1) Confirm what the requester actually needs
- Ask whether they will accept just the last 4 digits. Many organizations do.
- If they insist on the full SSN, ask for a secure upload portal or instructions for secure transmission.

2) If you can send only the last 4 digits
- Redact (black-out) the rest of the number so only the last four (6789) are visible.
- Remove SSN from the filename and email subject.

3) If you must send the full SSN, use secure methods
- Preferred: organization’s secure upload portal (HIPAA/PCI-compliant or equivalent).
- If uploading isn’t available: send a password-protected PDF (256-bit AES) or an encrypted archive (.zip/.7z) and deliver the password separately (phone or SMS).
- Better: encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) if both parties support it.

4) How to redact correctly (digital)
- Use a true redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro has a Redact tool) that permanently removes the text, not just covers it with a white box.
- After redaction, save as a new file, then verify you cannot select/copy the redacted text.
- Sanitize the document to remove metadata/hidden content.
- Alternate: convert the document to an image (export to TIFF/JPEG), crop/black out the SSN area, then convert back to PDF — this prevents underlying text extraction.
- Do NOT rely on drawing a white rectangle over the text in a PDF editor or PowerPoint—those are often reversible.

5) How to redact correctly (physical paper)
- Black permanent marker across digits, then make a photocopy of the blacked-out paper and scan the copy. This prevents ink bleed-through revealing the numbers.
- Verify the scan shows only the redaction, not the underlying numbers.

6) Extra safety steps
- Remove SSN from the email subject and the attached filename.
- Avoid uploading to public or shared folders unless they are secured and access-controlled.
- If you must email, attach a password-protected file and send the password separately.
- Consider setting expiration or restricted access if using cloud shares.
- Delete local copies when no longer needed and empty the recycle bin; check organization policy for record retention.

7) Verify redaction
- Open the final file and try to select/copy the redacted area; if you can copy the SSN or it appears in document text, the redaction is not secure.
- If possible, have the recipient confirm receipt via a secure channel.

Suggested short message to send the requester
- “Can you confirm whether you need the full SSN or if the last four digits (6789) are sufficient? If you require the full SSN, please provide a secure upload link or confirm how you’d like it transmitted.”

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short email asking the requester whether they accept last-4 or to provide a secure upload link.
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting a specific PDF (tell me whether you’re on Windows or Mac and which PDF tool you have).

