Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — unless the recipient explicitly requires the full SSN and provides a secure submission method, redact everything except the last 4 digits before sending a scanned document. Never send the full SSN over unencrypted email or insecure channels.

Practical guidance

1) Confirm need and method
- Ask the requester whether they truly need the full SSN or if the last four will do.
- Ask how they want it submitted (secure portal, encrypted email, secure upload, in person, fax). Follow their secure option.

2) How to redact safely
- Show only the last four digits (e.g., XXX-XX-1234) on the scanned page when possible.
- Use a proper redaction tool — do NOT just draw a black box or highlight in a viewer and save, because that can sometimes be reversed or the underlying text left in the file.
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Use Tools > Redact, mark text, then Apply Redactions and save a new file.
  - macOS Preview: Preview’s black boxes may not remove underlying text reliably. Better to export to PDF, open in a dedicated PDF redaction tool (or use PDFpen/Adobe).
  - Windows alternatives: PDF-XChange, Foxit, or Acrobat’s Redact feature.
  - If editing an image (JPEG/PNG), permanently erase the numbers in an image editor (crop or paint over), then save a new file; verify text cannot be selected or searched.
- After redaction, flatten/print-to-PDF the file and reopen to confirm the redacted text cannot be selected or copied.

3) Remove metadata
- Remove document metadata and hidden data (properties, comments, OCR text layer).
- In Acrobat: File > Properties to review; use Remove Hidden Information / Sanitize.
- For images, strip EXIF data (many image editors or settings when exporting let you remove metadata).

4) Secure transmission
- Preferred: upload via the recipient’s secure portal or a dedicated encrypted upload link.
- If email is the only option: send a password-protected (encrypted) PDF and share the password by a different channel (phone or SMS).
- Avoid sending via public cloud links without password or via plain unencrypted email.

5) File naming and content
- Don’t include the SSN in the filename.
- Only include the page(s) that are necessary; crop or remove unrelated pages that contain sensitive info.

6) If you already sent the full SSN insecurely
- Contact the recipient, ask them to delete the insecure copy and request a secure resubmission method.
- Monitor credit and accounts for suspicious activity.
- Consider placing a fraud alert or credit freeze if you’re concerned.
- Review guidance from the FTC on identity theft for next steps.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to the requester asking for a secure upload method.
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting a scan on your phone, Windows PC, or Mac — tell me which device and app you have.

