Short answer: Yes — unless the form specifically requires the full SSN, only share the minimum (usually the last 4 digits). If you must send a scan that contains your full SSN, take steps to redact or protect it and send it only by a secure channel.

Important: I won’t repeat your SSN here. Below are practical, safe options and step-by-step guidance.

When to redact
- If the form only asks for the last four digits, redact everything except the last 4 before you send.
- If the recipient explicitly requires the full SSN (and explains why), do not send it by unencrypted email or an insecure channel.

How to redact safely (recommended order)
1. Confirm what’s required
   - Ask the requester whether they truly need the full SSN or if the last four will do.

2. Best digital redaction (if you must send full or partial SSN)
   - Use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” is ideal) — it permanently removes the selected text/pixels and can remove metadata.
   - After redaction, save/export a new file and verify the redacted area cannot be selected or copied.
   - Remove any metadata: Acrobat has “Remove Hidden Information” / “Sanitize” options. If you don’t have Acrobat, print the redacted PDF to a new PDF (File > Print > Save as PDF) and verify.

3. Simple free methods to mask before scanning
   - On paper: black out the unwanted digits with a thick permanent marker, then photocopy or re-scan the paper copy to make sure the text underneath is not visible. (Scanning the original after only using a marker can sometimes still reveal underlying text if the marker is not opaque enough.)
   - Digital image editor: open the scan in an image editor and paint a solid black box over the digits, then save a flattened image/PDF so the hidden text cannot be recovered.

4. Avoid “just covering” in a PDF viewer (not safe)
   - Placing a black box annotation on top of the numbers in a PDF viewer without using a redaction tool can leave the original text in the file (searchable/selectable) and recoverable by others.

How to send the scan securely
- Use the requester’s secure portal or encrypted upload form (HTTPS). This is the best option.
- If you must email:
  - Send the redacted file only (preferably only last 4 visible).
  - Or send a password-protected PDF (strong password) and give the password over a separate channel (phone or SMS).
  - Better: use encrypted email or a secure file-sharing service (SFTP, ShareFile, OneDrive/Google Drive with link expiration and restricted access).
- Confirm with the recipient how they will store/limit access to your SSN.

If you’ve already sent the full SSN insecurely
- Contact the recipient immediately and ask them to delete the file and confirm deletion.
- Ask that they confirm secure receipt procedures for any re-submission.
- Monitor your credit and consider placing a fraud alert or credit freeze if you’re concerned.

Tools and quick tips
- Adobe Acrobat Pro: Redact tool + Sanitize.
- Mac Preview: edit image and export; better to flatten by printing to PDF after editing.
- Windows: Paint or any image editor to black box, then save as image or PDF.
- Free redaction tools: “PDF Redactor” or online redaction tools—be cautious using online services for SSNs.
- After redaction, open the PDF and try to select or copy the redacted area—if text is selectable, it’s not safe.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a message to the recipient asking whether they accept only the last four or a secure upload? 
- Walk you through redacting a specific file step-by-step (tell me whether you’re on Windows or macOS and which apps you have)?

