Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — avoid sending the full SSN unless absolutely required. If a form or recipient only needs the last four, redact the rest before you send a scan. Never put the full SSN in an email subject, filename, or unencrypted message.

Practical guidance and checklist

1) Decide whether the recipient really needs the full SSN
- Ask the requester: do you need the full SSN or will the last four digits suffice? Many organizations only require the last four for verification.
- If they insist on the full SSN, ask for a secure upload portal or encrypted channel.

2) Redaction basics (what to remove)
- Remove the first five digits; leave only the last four if that’s all they need (you told me the last four are 6789).
- Remove any copies, thumbnails, or OCR text layers that might contain the full number.
- Don’t rely on white-out or a black rectangle drawn in a viewer that doesn’t permanently remove underlying text.

3) How to redact safely (PDFs and images)
- Best: use a proper redaction tool that permanently deletes content and metadata (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool).
- On Mac: use Preview to export the image, draw a filled black rectangle and then Export as a new PDF, OR convert the page to a flattened image (Export → JPEG/PNG) and create a new PDF from that image. After flattening, open and search to confirm the full number cannot be found.
- On Windows (no Acrobat): print to PDF after placing a black box over the number in an image editor, or use a reputable PDF editor that supports permanent redaction.
- For scans saved as images: open in an image editor, paint over the digits to remove them, save as a new file (JPEG/PNG), then convert to PDF if required.
