- If you don’t have to send the full SSN, redact everything except the last four.
- Always use a secure method to send any document that contains a full SSN.

How to redact safely (options)
1. Physical redaction (very safe)
   - On the paper form, black out the unwanted digits with an opaque permanent marker.
   - Scan the paper after the ink is fully dry and send the scan.
   - Don’t just cover with sticky notes — the scanner may capture underlying text.

2. Digital redaction (recommended when working with PDFs)
   - Use a true redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool, PDFpen, PDFelement, etc.) that removes underlying text rather than just hiding it.
   - Steps (Adobe Acrobat Pro example): Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction (select digits) → Apply Redactions → Save As a new file.
   - After redaction, test by trying to select/copy the removed text or performing OCR; if you can’t see/select it, redaction likely succeeded.

3. Image-based redaction
   - Convert the page to an image (screenshot or export), permanently paint over the digits with a solid color, save the image, then convert back to PDF. This ensures the removed text is not recoverable as text.
   - Note: Converting back to a searchable PDF via OCR could re-create text, so avoid OCR after redaction.

4. Avoid insecure “visual only” methods
   - Don’t just place a white box or black rectangle in a PDF without using a redaction feature — the underlying text can often be recovered.

Sanitizing the file and metadata
- Remove metadata and hidden layers (Acrobat: Redact → Remove Hidden Information / Sanitize).
- Save as a new file name that does NOT contain the SSN.
- If you used an image editor, re-save as a flattened PDF (no layers).

Transport security (how to send)
- Best: upload to the recipient’s secure portal (employers/banks often provide these).
- If you must email, use end-to-end encryption (S/MIME or PGP) or send a password‑protected PDF (strong password) and share the password via a different channel (phone or text).
- Do not put SSN in the email subject or filename.
- Avoid sending full SSN over unencrypted email, SMS, or chat.

Verifying redaction worked
- Open the final file and try to select/copy the redacted digits. If selectable or OCR-recognizable, redaction failed.
- Open the file’s properties/metadata to confirm no hidden text remains.

Retention and minimize exposure
- Only send the pages that are necessary (e.g., a single page that shows the SSN rather than the whole file).
- Ask the recipient if they will delete the copy after processing, or request secure storage.
- Remove or securely delete local copies you no longer need.

