Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — avoid sending your full Social Security number in an unprotected scan unless the recipient absolutely needs it and you use a secure channel. Only share the full SSN when required; otherwise redact (or only show) the minimum (usually the last four).

Practical guidance and checklist

1) Confirm what the recipient actually needs
- Ask whether they need the full SSN or just the last four digits. If a form explicitly requests the full SSN, they may need the whole number; if not, provide only the last four.

2) Prefer secure upload over email
- Use the organization’s secure portal, encrypted upload, or an in-person delivery. These are the safest ways to give full SSNs.

3) If you must send a scan by email, protect it
- Password-protect the PDF (use a strong password) or encrypt the file.
- Send the password by a different channel (phone call or SMS).
- Consider using end-to-end encrypted email (S/MIME or similar) if available.

4) How to redact correctly
- Physical redaction before scanning: black out the SSN with a permanent marker, then scan the redacted paper. This is simple and effective.
- Digital redaction (best if done correctly): use a true redaction tool (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark → Apply, then Sanitize to remove hidden content). Proper redaction removes the underlying text and metadata — do not just place a white or black rectangle on top of live text, because the underlying text may still be recoverable.
- For image files, flatten the image (save/export to a new file) after redacting so edits can’t be reversed.
- Remove metadata/hidden data (Document Properties, metadata, comments, previous versions).

5) Other privacy tips
- Only include other required PII (DOB, address, etc.) — omit anything unnecessary.
- Verify the recipient’s email address and identity before sending.
- Avoid sending SSNs over SMS, regular (unencrypted) email, or public file-sharing links.
- Keep a record of who you gave it to and why; ask how they will store/secure it and for how long.
- Shred physical copies you no longer need.

6) If you listed different digits by mistake
- I noticed two different sets of last-four digits in your message. If you want step-by-step help that references the last four, please confirm which four digits are correct — you can type just the last four.

If you tell me how you plan to send the scan (email, upload portal, fax, etc.) and what software you have (Windows/Mac and Acrobat, Preview, or other), I can give specific step-by-step redaction or password-protection instructions.

