Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: In most cases you should redact (mask or remove) everything except the last four digits before sending a scanned document, unless the recipient specifically requires the full SSN and provides a secure way to send it.

What to do (quick checklist)
- Ask the recipient first: confirm whether they actually need the full SSN or if the last 4 digits will do. If they require the full number, ask what secure method they prefer (secure portal, encrypted upload, in-person).
- Mask the number on the document: hide all but the last four digits (for example: XXX-XX-6789).
- Avoid including the SSN in the email subject line, file name, or any visible metadata.
- Send via a secure channel: a secure upload portal, an encrypted email service, or password-protected PDF (share the password by a separate channel, e.g., SMS or phone).
- Keep the original document safe and log who you sent the copy to and when.

How to redact properly (so it can’t be recovered)
- PDF (recommended): use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact, or another PDF redaction feature). Mark the digits and Apply Redactions, then Save As a new file. This permanently removes the redacted text.
- Image scans: if you edit the image, permanently paint/black-bar the digits and then flatten and save a new image file (not just a black box over selectable text). Re-OCR the saved file to confirm the redaction can’t be selected.
- Physical copy: draw a thick, permanent black marker over the SSN on the paper, then scan the marked paper. Verify the blacked-out area cannot be read in the scan (and not just covered by a layer).
- Check metadata: strip metadata/EXIF from images or PDFs before sending.

When full SSN might be required
- Some government, tax, or identity-verification processes may legally require the full SSN. Employers, background-check vendors, the SSA, and IRS sometimes do. If they ask for the full SSN, insist on a secure transmission method.

Extra precautions
- Don’t put the SSN in file names (e.g., avoid “tax-SSN-123-45-6789.pdf”).
- If you must email, use encrypted email or password-protected attachments and send the password separately.
- Keep a copy of what you sent and confirmation that the recipient received it.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the recipient asking whether the last four digits are sufficient and what secure method they prefer.
- Walk you through redacting a PDF or removing metadata step-by-step—tell me what software you have (Adobe Acrobat, Preview on Mac, Windows, etc.).

Note: I won’t store or repeat your full SSN.

