- Best practice: remove or obscure all but the last four digits. If a form only asks for the last four, send only those.
- If the recipient specifically requires the full SSN (e.g., some tax, payroll, or background-check forms), confirm they provide a secure upload method before sending.

2) How to redact correctly (do NOT just draw a black box on top of text then send the original file)
- PDF (recommended): use a dedicated redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool or similar). Apply the redaction so the underlying text and metadata are permanently removed, then save a new file.
- Image/photos: open in an editor, place an opaque box over the digits to redact, then flatten/export as a new image (JPEG or PDF). After saving, re-open the file to confirm the redacted content is gone.
- Physical documents: cover the unwanted digits with opaque tape or paper, then photocopy or re-scan the covered copy (not the original marked sheet).
- Verify: try selecting/copying text from the saved file to ensure the redacted digits can’t be recovered; check/remove metadata/EXIF.

3) Secure transmission
- Use the recipient’s secure portal or upload form whenever available.
- If you must send a file, use an encrypted method: send a password-protected PDF (strong password) and transmit the password separately (e.g., call or SMS).
- Avoid sending full SSNs over regular email, SMS, or chat unless the connection is encrypted and the recipient expects it.
- If the organization asks for the SSN by email, ask them to provide a secure alternative (portal, secure email, or fax).

4) If you must provide the full SSN
- Confirm why it’s needed, how it will be stored, who will have access, and how long they will keep it.
- Insist on a secure submission channel and request confirmation of secure handling.

5) Tools and cautions
- Good tools: Adobe Acrobat Pro redaction, PDF Expert, Windows built-in Paint + Print-to-PDF (but ensure flattening), trusted company portals.
- Don’t upload sensitive docs to unknown online redaction websites — they may store copies.

