Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: only share the full SSN if the recipient explicitly requires it and the transfer is done securely. Otherwise redact everything except the last four digits.

Practical guidance and steps

1) Before you send anything
- Ask the recipient whether the full SSN is required or whether the last 4 digits will do. Many forms and organizations accept just the last four.
- Confirm a secure method for submission (secure portal, encrypted upload) — avoid regular email/SMS when sending full SSNs.

2) How to redact a scanned document safely
- Use a true redaction tool that removes underlying text/metadata (not just hides it visually).
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Sanitize Document → Save a copy.
  - PDF editors with a “redact” or “secure erase” feature (PDF Expert, Nitro, etc.) work similarly.
- If you don’t have a PDF redaction tool:
  - Convert the page to an image (take a screenshot or export to JPG), open in an image editor, draw a solid black box over the SSN, then save a new file and convert back to PDF if needed. This removes searchable text.
  - Or print the page, black out the SSN with a thick permanent marker (confirm it’s fully opaque), then scan the blacked-out page. Don’t just cover with tape/whiteout — rescanning the original unredacted text may still be recoverable.
- Never redact by placing a black rectangle with a PDF annotation-only tool that can be removed or that leaves the original text behind. Always use a redaction function or create a flattened image file.

3) Metadata and searchable text
- After redacting, verify the SSN is not present in underlying text or metadata. Use “Save As…” or export to a new flattened PDF/image. Some redaction tools offer “Sanitize” to remove metadata.
- For images, strip EXIF data before sending (many phone photo apps and scanners include location/metadata).

4) Sending securely
- Prefer the recipient’s secure portal or an encrypted email service.
- If you must email the file, password‑protect the PDF (and choose a strong password) and share the password by phone or a separate channel—not in the same email.
- Avoid putting SSNs in filenames or in the email subject line.
- Confirm receipt by contacting the recipient via a trusted phone number.

5) Physical copies and disposal
- If you printed anything with the full SSN, shred or securely store it when finished.
- If you sent a physical copy, consider using tracked/certified mail if appropriate.

6) When full SSN might be required
- Some government forms, background checks, tax documents, or benefits applications may legally require the full SSN. If that’s the case, request exact instructions for secure submission.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to the recipient asking whether the last four digits are sufficient and which secure method they prefer.
- Give step-by-step redaction instructions for a specific tool or device (e.g., Adobe Acrobat on Windows, Preview or macOS, iPhone scanning apps). Tell me which you use and I’ll walk you through it.

Note: I didn’t repeat the number you typed for your safety — I recommend following the steps above to keep it protected.

