I won’t repeat your SSN here. Also: I noticed the full number and the “last four” you wrote don’t match — if you want specific help about which digits to include, please confirm which is correct. Below are safe, practical guidelines for sending a scanned document that contains an SSN.

Quick rules
- Never send a full SSN over unencrypted email or in an email subject/filename/preview.
- It’s usually fine to share only the last four digits when that is all the form requires.
- If the recipient truly requires the full SSN, use a secure submission method (secure portal/upload, encrypted email, fax, or in-person/mailed copy).

If you must send a scan with the full SSN — safer options
1. Use the recipient’s secure upload portal (preferred).
2. If you must email: encrypt the file (password-protect the PDF) and send the password separately (e.g., by phone or SMS).
3. Use encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP), or an enterprise secure file transfer service.
4. Physical mail with tracking or hand-delivery if digital security options aren’t available.

How to redact properly (so the SSN is unrecoverable)
- Best: use a dedicated redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact tool or another trusted PDF redaction tool).
  Steps (Adobe Acrobat Pro): Tools > Redact > Mark for Redaction (select the SSN) > Apply Redactions > File > Save As (save new file) > Sanitize Document.
- If you don’t have a redaction tool: print the document, permanently black out the SSN with a heavy marker, then scan/photograph the redacted paper into a new file. (Do not just place a black box over text in a PDF and save — that can leave the underlying text searchable/recoverable unless you flatten/sanitize the file.)
- For image scans/photos: remove EXIF/metadata before sending (many image editors or dedicated metadata removal tools can do this).
- After redaction: verify the SSN is not recoverable by searching the saved file for digits, trying to select text, and checking file properties.

Other practical precautions
- Don’t put SSN digits in the file name, email subject, or message preview.
- Limit who receives the file; confirm recipient’s email/address and identity.
- Keep an audit trail (who you sent it to and when), then delete the local copy if you don’t need it.
- Consider asking the recipient if they’ll accept just the last four or an alternative ID so you can avoid sharing the full SSN.

Suggested short message to a recipient when you want a secure option
“Can you confirm a secure way to submit my SSN? I can upload via your secure portal or send an encrypted PDF — what do you prefer? I’d rather not email the full number unencrypted.”

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short email asking the organization for a secure upload option.
- Give step-by-step redaction instructions for a specific program (e.g., Adobe Acrobat, Preview on Mac, or Windows tools).
- Help confirm which digits you intended to provide (you can clarify which four are correct).

