Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Only share the full SSN if the recipient explicitly requires it and they provide a secure way to receive it. Otherwise, send only the last four digits (you mentioned last four are 6789). If you must send a scan that contains the full SSN, redact or secure it first.

Practical guidance and steps

1) Confirm what they actually need
- Ask the recipient whether the last 4 digits will suffice or whether the full SSN is required for the form. If they require the full SSN, ask for a secure upload method (secure portal, encrypted email, or secure file transfer).

2) Redaction vs. masking — what works and what doesn’t
- True redaction permanently removes the underlying text/characters. Use a proper redaction tool so the SSN cannot be recovered.
- Do NOT rely on simple “white boxes” or drawing black rectangles in a viewer that only overlays the image; searchable text or metadata can remain and be recovered.
- A black marker on the paper can work if you physically destroy any copies with the unredacted number before sending—but it’s safer to redact digitally after scanning with a trusted tool.

3) How to redact a scanned document safely
- Best: Use a PDF editor with a redact/sanitize feature (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark → Apply; then Sanitize Document to remove metadata). After applying redaction, save as a new file and verify the numbers are not selectable or searchable.
- If the document is just an image: open in an image editor, permanently delete the digits, flatten/merge layers, save as a new file, then convert to PDF. Confirm OCR/search won’t reveal the removed numbers.
- Avoid free phone apps that automatically upload to cloud (unless you control the upload and storage).

4) Remove metadata and OCR text
- After redaction, sanitize the file to remove hidden metadata, embedded OCR text layers, comments, or previous document revisions.
- Verify by trying to search the PDF for the SSN digits or by selecting the area where the SSN was; nothing should be selectable or searchable.

5) Secure transmission
- Preferred: upload via the recipient’s secure portal.
- If you must email: password-protect the PDF (use AES encryption) and send the password by a separate channel (phone call or text). Better: use an encrypted email (S/MIME/PGP) or a secure file transfer service.
- Avoid sending full SSN in a plain email body or insecure attachments.

6) What else to redact
- Consider redacting other sensitive identifiers you don’t need to share: date of birth, driver’s license, account numbers, etc., unless required by the form.

7) After sending
- Ask the recipient to confirm receipt and deletion of any copies they don’t need. Keep minimal copies on your side and delete temporary files securely.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to the recipient asking whether full SSN is required and requesting a secure upload method.
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting a specific file (tell me what OS and software you have—e.g., Adobe Acrobat, Preview on Mac, Windows tools).

