Short answer: yes — don’t send the full SSN in an ordinary email or in an un-redacted scan unless the recipient explicitly requires it and provides a secure way to upload it. Only share the minimum (often the last 4 digits) and use a secure channel when the full SSN is required.

A few practical points and steps:

1. Don’t repost the full SSN here
- I noticed you included two different numbers in your message. For your safety, please don’t paste your full SSN into chat or regular email. If you want me to refer to the last four, tell me only the last four digits.

2. Confirm what the recipient actually needs
- Many organizations only need the last four digits for verification. Check the form’s instructions or contact the requester and ask whether they require the full SSN or if the last four will suffice.

3. If last four digits are acceptable
- Redact (mask) the rest of the number on the scan so only the last four are visible. That is usually safe to send by email.

4. If the full SSN is required, use secure transmission
- Preferred methods: the organization’s secure upload portal, an encrypted email (S/MIME, PGP), or a secure file-transfer service with link expiration and access control.
- If you must email a file, encrypt or password-protect the PDF and transmit the password separately (e.g., by phone or SMS).

5. How to redact properly (so underlying text isn’t recoverable)
- Best: use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro’s “Redact” feature or another PDF redaction tool) which removes underlying text and metadata.
- Free/safer alternative: open the file as an image (or convert PDF page to image), draw a solid black box over the digits, flatten/save a new file (so the text layer is removed), then save as a new PDF/JPEG.
- Avoid just drawing a black box in a program that leaves the original selectable text underneath — that can be recovered.
- After redaction, check by trying to select/copy text or by searching the PDF; the redacted digits should not be present in the text layer.

6. Remove metadata and hidden info
- Use “Remove hidden information” / “Sanitize” features where available, or save as a flattened image to avoid embedded metadata or OCR text.

7. Double-check all occurrences
- Make sure you’ve redacted every place the SSN appears (front/back, headers, embedded text, email body, file name, EXIF metadata).

8. Quick checklist to follow before sending
- Confirm recipient accepts last four only (if so, redact the rest).
- Use a proper redaction tool or flatten the image.
- Remove metadata / sanitize.
- Send via secure portal or encrypt the file.
- If you must email, password-protect the file and share the password separately.
- Ask recipient to confirm secure receipt and then delete the transmitted copy if policy requires.

9. Example email you can send when attaching a redacted scan
- “Attached is a copy of [document], with my SSN masked to show only the last four digits as requested. If you need the full SSN, please confirm and provide a secure upload method or instructions for transmitting it securely.”

If you want, tell me:
- Whether the form specifically requests the full SSN or only the last four (don’t paste the full SSN here), and
- How you plan to send the scan (email, portal, etc.),

and I can give step‑by‑step instructions for redacting and securely sending the file or draft the short email text for you.

