Short answer: yes — redact anything beyond the minimum the recipient needs, and send full SSNs only by a secure method. I noticed a mismatch in what you typed (the “last four” you listed doesn’t match the end of the nine‑digit SSN you wrote) — which is correct?

Guidance and practical steps

1) Decide what’s required
- If the form only asks for the last 4 digits, send only the last 4. Do NOT include the full SSN.
- If the form requires the full SSN, ask whether there’s a secure upload portal or encrypted method before sending.

2) How to redact safely (digital files)
- Best: start with a PDF and use a proper redaction tool that permanently removes content (not just hides it).
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Save as a new file. Then use “Sanitize Document” / “Remove Hidden Information.”
  - Other PDF editors with redaction functions (PDF XChange, Nitro, etc.) follow similar workflows.
- Free / alternative approach (if you don’t have a redaction tool):
  - Convert the document to an image (scan or export pages as PNG/JPG).
  - Use an image editor (Paint, Preview, GIMP) to draw a solid black box over digits to remove.
  - Flatten/merge layers and save a new image file, then create a PDF from that image.
  - Verify by opening the saved file and trying to select/copy text — the redacted digits should not be selectable or searchable.
- Avoid simply drawing white boxes or “hiding” text without flattening/exporting — hidden text or metadata may still contain the SSN.

3) If you must redact a scanned paper copy
- Better: scan the full document, redact digitally as above, then save a new file and securely send that.
- If you try to redact on the paper original (black marker/opaque tape) before scanning, be sure the ink/tape is truly opaque and the scan shows nothing underneath; digital redaction after scanning is safer.

4) Remove hidden data / OCR layers / metadata
- After redacting, remove metadata and embedded text layers. In Acrobat this is “Sanitize Document.” In images, ensure you saved a flattened image (no hidden layers).
- Search the file for the SSN digits to confirm they aren’t present.

5) Sending securely
- Preferred: upload to a secure recipient portal or use an encrypted transfer service.
- If emailing: password‑protect the PDF (use a strong password) and send the password by a different channel (text or phone). Or use an encrypted email service (S/MIME or provider-built encryption).
- Confirm the recipient’s email address and whether they have a secure upload option.

6) Extra precautions
- Only include other identifiers (DOB, driver’s license) if required.
- Keep a minimal number of copies and store them securely.
- If in doubt, call the organization and ask: “Do you need the full SSN or only the last 4? How should I upload it securely?”

