Short answer: Yes — don’t include your full SSN in a regular scanned document or unsecured email unless it’s absolutely required and you use a secure channel. In most cases you should only share the last four digits (which you noted as 6789) and redact the rest.

Guidance and actionable steps

1) Before you send anything
- Confirm what the recipient actually needs: sometimes the form only requires the last 4 digits. Ask if last-4 will suffice.
- Ask the recipient for a secure submission method (secure portal, encrypted upload, or fax). Avoid sending a full SSN by plain/unencrypted email.

2) If you only need to supply last 4 digits
- Redact or mask the other numbers so the final scan only shows “***-**-6789” (or similar).
- It’s fine to type the last four into the form or to send a scanned/photographed page where the other digits are permanently redacted.

3) How to redact correctly (don’t just cover it with a white box that can be removed)
- Physical method: print the page, permanently black out the unwanted digits with a thick marker, then rescan or photograph the blacked-out page.
- Digital method (preferred):
  - Use a proper PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool or equivalent). Mark the SSN and apply redaction — that tool removes the underlying content, not just hides it.
  - If using an image editor, crop out or permanently delete the digits, then save as a new file.
  - After redaction, verify you cannot select or search the redacted text (try copy/paste or Search).
  - Remove document metadata (File → Properties → Remove personal info) if possible.
  - Save a new file; do not rely on layers or annotation that can be toggled off.

4) If the full SSN is required
- Use a secure channel: encrypted upload portal, secure file-transfer (SFTP), or a confirmed fax/mailed copy or in-person delivery.
- If you must email, encrypt the file (password-protect the PDF with strong password/AES-256) and send the password by a separate channel (SMS or phone call).
- Confirm retention policy and request deletion if they don’t need to keep the copy.

5) Extra safety tips
- Verify the recipient’s identity and email address before sending.
- Don’t include the full SSN in the email body or subject line.
- Keep a copy of what you sent and note who you sent it to and when.
- If you ever suspect misuse, monitor your credit and consider a fraud alert or freeze.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the organization asking for a secure upload option, or
- Walk you through redacting a PDF on Windows/Mac/phone (tell me which device and app you have).

