Short answer
- If the recipient only needs to verify identity, usually providing just the last four digits (e.g., XXX-XX-6789) is sufficient. 
- If they explicitly require the full SSN, don’t send it by plain email or an unsecured channel—use their secure upload portal or a password‑protected/encrypted file and confirm how they prefer to receive it.

What to redact when sending a scan
- If full SSN is not required: redact all but the last four digits (display as XXX-XX-6789 or *****6789).  
- If full SSN is required: send only through a secure method (see below). If you must include it in a scan, make sure the file is permanently redacted or encrypted before sending.

How to permanently redact (desktop and mobile options)
- Best (permanent) method: use a PDF editor with a Redact tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro, PDFpen, PDF XChange). Steps: Mark text to redact → Apply redactions → Save a new file. This removes the underlying text so it cannot be recovered.
- If you don’t have a redaction tool:
  - Convert the scanned PDF page to an image (or take a high-resolution photo).
  - Use an image editor to paint a solid black box over the digits to remove, then save/export as a new flattened image or PDF. (Flattening ensures the covered text isn’t still embedded.)
  - On Mac: you can use Preview + export as PDF after drawing the box, but exporting/flattening is important to avoid recoverable text.
  - On iPhone/Android: use a scanning app that supports permanent redaction or edit the photo, draw a solid black box, then export/save as a new image/PDF.
- Avoid non‑destructive overlays (e.g., adding a black box in Word or a PDF viewer that leaves the original text intact). Those can be removed and reveal the data.

Remove metadata
- After redacting, remove file metadata/EXIF (scan software or image editors can do this; some OSes have “remove properties” features). This prevents hidden data like GPS or original file history from leaking.

Secure transmission
- Preferred: upload via the recipient’s secure portal (encrypted) or use encrypted email/secure file transfer.
- If you must email: create a password‑protected PDF (use Acrobat or similar), send the file by email, then give the password by a different channel (phone or SMS).
- Don’t include the full SSN in the email body or an unencrypted attachment.

Quick checklist before sending
- Confirm whether the recipient accepts last four only.
- If last four is acceptable: redact the rest and save as a new, flattened file.
- If full SSN is required: use the recipient’s secure portal or send a permanently redacted/encrypted file and transmit the password separately.
- Remove metadata.
- Keep a copy of what you sent and how so you can track.

Sample message to the recipient (you can send to ask what they need)
“Hi — I’m preparing to send a scan that contains my SSN. Do you need the full SSN or are the last four digits sufficient? If you need the full SSN, what secure upload method do you prefer?”

