Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually you should NOT send the full SSN in an ordinary email or unprotected scan. Only share the full SSN when absolutely required and then use a secure channel (secure upload portal, encrypted email, in-person, or fax). If the form only needs the last four digits, send only those.

What to do when sending a scan (recommended checklist)
- Confirm whether the recipient really needs the full SSN. Ask them if the last four digits are sufficient or if there’s a secure upload option.
- If full SSN is NOT required, redact everything except the last four digits before sending.
- Use proper, permanent redaction — don’t just hide text with a white box or cover it visually without removing the underlying data.
- Use a secure transfer method: secure web portal, encrypted email, or password-protected PDF (send the password over a different channel, e.g., SMS or phone).
- Remove metadata/hidden layers before sending (see steps below).
- If you must email, password-protect the file and confirm the recipient’s address and identity first.
- Keep a copy of the redacted file and securely delete any unredacted copies you created for scanning.

How to redact correctly (digital and photo methods)
- Best (professional): Use a PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions; then Sanitize/Remove Hidden Information). This permanently removes the redacted content and any hidden text/OCR layers.
- If you don’t have Acrobat:
  - Turn the page into an image (take a photo or export page as a JPEG), open it in an image editor, black out the SSN pixels, save the image, then create a new PDF from that image. That ensures underlying text/OCR is gone.
  - Or use a trusted PDF editor with a “permanent redact” or “flatten” option. After redaction, export/print to a new PDF to flatten.
- If using Preview on Mac: place a black rectangle over the SSN, then export the document as a new PDF and verify the text cannot be selected/copy-pasted. (When in doubt, convert the page to an image first and rebuild the PDF from the image.)
- For paper scans: mark the SSN on the printed copy with a permanent black marker, then scan the marked paper. Verify the blacked-out area contains no underlying readable text in the scanned file.
- Always verify after redaction by trying to select/copy the redacted area or running a quick OCR search — make sure the numbers are gone.

Remove metadata and hidden content
- Use the “Remove Hidden Information” or “Sanitize” features in Acrobat if available.
- If you converted to image and rebuilt the PDF, that usually removes metadata and OCR layers.
- Check File Properties and remove author/phone/location data if present.

Secure sending tips
- Use the recipient’s secure upload portal whenever possible.
- If sending via email, attach a password-protected PDF and send the password separately.
- Avoid sending sensitive attachments over public Wi‑Fi.
- Confirm the recipient’s email address and that they expect the document.
- After confirmation, securely delete any unneeded copies from your device and cloud backups.

When you might have to send the full SSN
- Some official forms (tax, payroll, certain government or financial applications) do require the full SSN. If that’s the case, ask the organization for their preferred secure submission method (portal, in-person, fax, encrypted email).

