Short answer: only share the minimum required (usually the last 4 digits). If you must send a scan that contains your full SSN, redact (permanently remove) everything except what the recipient explicitly needs, and send the file over a secure channel.

A practical checklist and safe methods:

1. Confirm what’s required
- Ask the requester whether they actually need the full SSN or if the last four will do. Many forms/organizations accept just the last four.

2. If only the last four are required
- Crop the scan so only the necessary portion (the last four) is visible, or redact all other digits.
- Do not send the full number “just in case.”

3. Redaction — best practices (digital)
- Use a proper redaction tool (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact tool) that removes the text layer and any hidden text rather than just placing a black box on top.
- After redaction, “sanitize” or “flatten” the PDF so no hidden text or metadata remains.
- Open the final file and try selecting text or copying to ensure the SSN isn’t still recoverable.
- Remove metadata/EXIF from the file (many PDF tools and image editors can do this).

4. Redaction — best practices (paper)
- Permanently black out the digits with an opaque marker, then photocopy the redacted page and scan the photocopy. (Marker + photocopy reduces the chance of the underlying digits being recoverable.)
- Don’t use whiteout or a translucent cover that can be reversed.

5. Transmission security
- Use the organization’s secure upload portal whenever available (most banks, government offices, employers provide one).
- If you must email, use encrypted email or send a password-protected PDF and share the password by a separate channel (e.g., SMS or phone call).
- Avoid sending full SSNs over unencrypted email, standard chat apps, or public/shared drives.

6. File naming and storage
- Don’t put the SSN in the file name.
- Delete temporary files and empty your trash. Remove scans you don’t need from cloud backups.

7. Verify the result
- Before sending, confirm you cannot recover the redacted digits by opening the file, searching for digits, or copying text.
- If possible, ask the recipient to confirm they received only what’s needed.

8. If you’re unsure or it’s a legal/financial situation
- Contact the requesting organization and request a secure upload method or written confirmation of exactly what they need.

One note: I noticed the digits you wrote (the full number and the “last four”) don’t match; please double-check privately which digits you intend to send and avoid posting your full SSN in chat.

If you want, tell me how you plan to send the scan (email attachment, upload portal, fax, etc.) and I’ll give step‑by‑step instructions for that method.

