Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: only share the full Social Security number (SSN) when absolutely necessary. Whenever possible send only the last four digits. If you must send a scan that contains your SSN, use proper redaction and a secure transmission method.

What to do, step-by-step

1. Confirm what they really need
- Ask the requester whether the last four digits are sufficient. Many forms and organizations accept just the last four.
- If they insist on the full SSN, ask for a secure upload portal or other secure transmission method.

2. Prefer secure delivery over email
- Best: upload through the recipient’s secure portal (most banks/HR/payroll services provide one).
- If you must send electronically: use end-to-end encrypted email or a password‑protected PDF and share the password by a separate channel (text or phone).
- Avoid sending full SSN in plain, unencrypted email.

3. Redaction methods (safe → less safe)
- Best (digital redaction): Use a PDF redaction feature (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions). That removes the text from the PDF, not just hides it. Then use “Remove Hidden Information” / “Sanitize” to strip metadata and hidden text layers. Save as a new file.
- Alternate (paper-first): On the paper copy, thoroughly cover the SSN with a permanent black marker so digits are not visible, then scan the covered paper. This physically prevents OCR from capturing the digits. If you do this, be sure the ink fully obscures characters.
- Avoid: simply drawing a black rectangle on a scanned PDF or placing an annotation box that only visually hides text. That often leaves the underlying text or OCR layer intact and recoverable.

4. Flatten and remove metadata
- After redaction, flatten the PDF (so any layers are merged) and remove metadata/hidden text. In Acrobat use “Save as” or the “Flatten”/“Sanitize” tools. On Mac/Windows, printing to PDF after proper redaction can help remove selectable text layers, but only if the redaction truly removed the content first.
- Check the file by searching the PDF for your SSN (or last four) — if the search finds it, redaction was not effective.

5. Verify redaction worked
- Try selecting the redacted area; if you can select or copy the numbers, the redaction failed.
- Use a PDF reader’s “Find” to search for the full number and last four digits. If they appear, redo redaction.

6. If you must send the full SSN
- Use secure portal or encrypted email.
- Limit who receives it and include a brief note stating why it’s needed.
- Keep a record of when/why you shared it and with whom.

7. Protect the original
- If you blacked out a paper copy, keep or dispose of the original according to your security preference (shred if you don’t need it). If you created a version containing the SSN that you no longer need, delete securely.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message asking the recipient whether the last four digits are sufficient or requesting a secure upload link.
- Give platform-specific redaction steps (Windows, macOS, iPhone/Android, or specific PDF tools).
Tell me which you'd like and I’ll help.

