Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — avoid sending the full SSN in a scan unless the recipient explicitly requires the full number and you can transmit it securely. In most cases it’s safest to redact everything except the last four digits (or to mask the entire number).

Practical guidance and best practices

1. Confirm what the recipient actually needs
- Ask the organization whether they need the full SSN or only the last 4. Many forms only require the last 4.
- If they insist on the full SSN, ask for their preferred secure submission method (secure upload portal, encrypted email, in-person, or secure fax).

2. How to redact before or after scanning
- Physical redaction (before scanning): use a permanent black marker to fully block the digits you don’t want shown, then scan. This is reliable because the hidden digits are removed from the paper image.
- Digital redaction (after scanning): use a proper PDF redaction tool (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” feature or other PDF editors with a redaction function). Redaction tools remove the underlying content — do NOT just draw a black box or white rectangle on the image because that can leave the original data recoverable.
- If you use image editors, rasterize the image (convert to a flattened image) and then permanently paint over the digits, then save as a new file. Verify there is no selectable/searchable text behind the concealment.

3. Remove metadata and hidden data
- After redacting, create a brand-new file and remove metadata (EXIF, file comments). Many PDF editors have a “sanitize” or “remove hidden information” feature.
- Verify the redacted region cannot be selected or copied.

4. Secure transmission
- Use the recipient’s secure portal whenever possible.
- If you must email, send a password-protected PDF (strong password) and share the password by a separate channel (phone or SMS).
- Avoid sending full SSN over plain/unencrypted email or public Wi‑Fi.
- If sending by fax, confirm the correct fax number and recipient name.

5. Keep copies and limit exposure
- Keep the scanned file only as long as necessary; delete local copies when no longer needed.
- Shred physical paperwork you no longer need.

6. Exceptions / legal context
- Some official processes (tax transcripts, background checks, government benefits, banking) legitimately require an unredacted SSN. In those cases, follow the organization’s instructions and insist on a secure method to submit it.

7. Suggested redaction formats (examples)
- Fully masked: XXX-XX-XXXX
- Masked except last 4: XXX-XX-1234
(Replace 1234 with the actual last four only when needed — I didn’t repeat your number here.)

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message asking the recipient what exactly they require and how to submit it securely.
- Walk you through redacting a scanned PDF on Windows, macOS, or with a specific app (tell me which OS or app you have).

