- If you must send a scan showing the full SSN, redact everything except the minimum required (or use a secure upload method and encrypt the file).

Detailed guidance and steps

1) Confirm what the recipient actually needs
- Ask them: “Do you need the full SSN on a scan, a copy of my SSN card, or will the last four digits suffice?” Many organizations accept the last four for verification.

2) If you can provide only the last four
- Mask the rest (e.g., XXX-XX-6789) or crop the document so only the last four remain visible.
- Don’t put the SSN in the filename or email subject.

3) If you must send a scan showing a full SSN — safest approach
- Use the recipient’s secure portal (recommended) or request an encrypted upload link.
- If portal isn’t available, create an encrypted PDF:
  - Use an editor (Adobe Acrobat, Preview+third-party, or PDF utility) to password-protect the PDF with strong encryption (AES-256).
  - Send the password via a different channel (text or phone), not in the same email.

4) How to redact properly (don’t just cover with an annotation/white box)
- Digital redaction (preferred)
  - Use a real redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools > Redact; mark text; Apply Redactions). This permanently removes the underlying text/pixels.
  - If using another PDF editor, use its official “redact” feature — not just a black rectangle annotation.
  - After redaction, save as a new file, then test: try to select/copy the redacted area or run OCR — the SSN should be gone.
  - Remove metadata and hidden layers (File > Properties or “Remove hidden information” features), then flatten the PDF if possible.
- Image-based redaction
  - Convert the page to an image, paint a solid black box over the SSN in an image editor (GIMP, Paint, Preview), save as a new image, then re-create a PDF from that image. This ensures pixels are removed rather than covered by an overlay.
- Physical redaction (if you must hand-redact)
  - Use an opaque permanent marker to black out the SSN on the original, then photocopy/scan the redacted original. Do not just place a sticky note or write over it and then scan the original unredacted.

5) Extra precautions
- Remove the SSN from the filename and any document properties.
- Keep a copy of what you sent and to whom, in case of future questions.
- If an organization truly needs proof of the SSN (payroll, benefits), they may request an SSN card or official document; ask which is acceptable.
- Beware of phishing: confirm the request is legitimate before sending sensitive documents.

