Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — unless the recipient explicitly requires the full number, redact everything except the last four digits. Share the minimum information needed and use a secure method to send it.

Why:
- A scanned/photographed document can be copied, OCR’d, and its contents recovered. Full SSNs are prime targets for identity theft.
- Many organizations only need the last four digits for identification; only give the full SSN if they specifically ask and provide a secure submission method.

How to redact safely (practical steps)
1. Confirm what they actually need
   - Ask the recipient whether they will accept “last 4 digits only” or whether the full SSN is required. Ask them to provide a secure upload portal or encrypted/email instructions if they need the full number.

2. If last four digits are sufficient
   - Cover or remove all other digits before scanning/photographing (e.g., place a slip of paper over the rest) OR redact the scanned file (see next).

3. Redacting PDFs (recommended)
   - Use a true redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact). This removes the text and metadata permanently.
   - If you don’t have Acrobat Pro: annotate with a solid black box, then “flatten” the PDF by printing to PDF (Print → “Microsoft Print to PDF” or “Save as PDF”), which removes the editable layer so underlying text can’t be recovered.
   - After redaction, check the file (search for any digit strings or run OCR) to confirm the SSN isn’t recoverable.

4. Redacting images (photos)
   - Open in an image editor (Paint, Preview, Photos) and use a solid fill to block digits, then save a new flattened image format (JPEG/PNG).
   - Re-check the image visually; avoid using white/transparent overlays that could be lifted.

5. Remove metadata
   - PDFs and images can include metadata. After saving the redacted, flattened file, remove metadata (many PDF editors and image apps offer this; on Windows, right‑click → Properties → Details → Remove Properties; on macOS use “Export” and uncheck metadata where available).

6. Protect transmission
   - Use the recipient’s secure portal if they provide one.
   - If emailing: password-protect the PDF (AES encryption), or send an encrypted ZIP. Share the password via a separate channel (e.g., text or phone).
   - Consider secure file transfer services or enterprise-approved encrypted email.

7. Keep minimal copies and log what you sent
   - Only keep what you need. If you must keep a copy, store it encrypted.
   - Consider deleting local copies after confirmation that the recipient received it.

Quick checklist before sending
- Did I confirm whether full SSN is required?
- If only last 4 required — did I redact the rest before scanning (or redact and flatten the file)?
- Did I remove metadata and verify no plain-text SSN remains?
- Am I sending via a secure portal or encrypted attachment?
- Did I avoid putting the SSN in the email body?

If you tell me what device and software you’re using (Windows, macOS, iPhone, Android; or which PDF app), I can give step-by-step redaction instructions or walk you through password-protecting the file. I can also draft a short message to the recipient asking whether they need the full SSN and what secure method they prefer.

