Short answer: Yes — redact everything except the minimum the recipient explicitly requires (usually just the last four digits). Don’t send the full SSN over an unsecured channel unless the form or organization explicitly requires it.

Practical guidance and checklist
- Confirm the requirement first
  - Ask the organization whether they truly need the full SSN or if the last 4 digits will suffice for the form or verification. If they need the full SSN, ask for a secure upload method (secure portal, encrypted email, SFTP).

- Common safe format
  - If only last 4 are required, show only those: XXX-XX-6789 (or just 6789). Do not include the full number anywhere.

- How to redact a physical document before scanning
  - Permanently obscure the unwanted digits on the paper (black permanent marker, then let it dry).
  - Do not use removable tape or light white-out that can be peeled off or scanned through.
  - Scan the redacted paper and confirm the covered digits are not visible in the scan.

- How to redact a digital/PDF file safely
  - Use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Redact → Mark → Apply Redactions). This removes the text from the file, not just hides it.
  - If you don’t have Acrobat: convert to an image (print-to-PDF or take a screenshot), use an image editor to paint over the digits with solid black, then save/export as a new PDF or image.
  - After redaction, flatten the PDF or save as a new file so layers aren’t recoverable.
  - Verify by trying to select/copy the redacted area — there should be no selectable text there.

- Mobile apps
  - Use a reputable scanner app that supports permanent redaction or export to PDF then edit with a secure redaction method. If unsure, mark on the paper and rescan (physical redaction is often simplest).

- Metadata and OCR
  - Make sure OCR hasn’t retained the redacted digits. Test by searching the PDF for the full SSN (if you can remember parts, don’t search too widely). Better: avoid keeping originals unredacted on your device.

- Transmission security
  - Prefer secure upload portals or encrypted email (or password‑protected PDF — share the password separately, e.g., by text or phone).
  - Avoid sending SSNs in plain email or SMS. If forced to email, password-protect the PDF and send the password in a separate channel.

- Keep records minimal and secure
  - Store only what you must. Delete or securely store unneeded copies of unredacted documents.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message asking the organization whether the last four digits are sufficient and what secure upload options they provide.
- Walk you through redacting step-by-step on your device (Windows/Mac/iPhone/Android) — tell me which you’re using.

Note: I won’t repeat or store the full SSN. You already noted the last four as 6789; use only that when possible.

