- If only last 4 are required: permanently redact (remove) the other digits so they cannot be recovered.
- If the full SSN is required: use a secure method to transmit (secure upload portal, encrypted email, password‑protected file shared separately, or hand delivery).
- Never send the full SSN in plain email, SMS, or an unencrypted cloud link.

How to redact correctly (avoid simple overlays)
- Use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro has a Redact tool that removes underlying text).
- If you don’t have a redaction tool: open the image/PDF in an editor, permanently paint/erase the SSN digits (not just place a black box layer), then “print to PDF” or export to a new PDF so the original text/image underneath is not recoverable.
- Do NOT rely on removable annotations or white-out. Avoid just placing a black shape over text in apps where the original can be extracted.
- After redaction, “flatten” or print-to-PDF the file to remove layers, and verify by opening the new file and trying to search/select the redacted text.

Remove metadata and copies
- Remove metadata or personal info from the file (file properties) if possible.
- Don’t leave the unredacted original attached or uploaded anywhere accessible.
- If you used cloud storage, use a secure link with expiration and limited access, or delete the file after the recipient confirms receipt.

Secure sending options
- Recipient’s secure upload portal (best when available).
- Encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) if both sides support it.
- Password‑protected PDF or an encrypted ZIP/7‑Zip archive; send password separately (phone, SMS, or verbally).
- Deliver in person or by secure courier if feasible.

