Short answer: yes — unless the recipient explicitly requires the full SSN and you're sending it through a secure channel, you should redact everything except the last four digits (you mentioned it ends in 6789). Here’s what to do and why.

1) Principle
- Only share the full SSN when absolutely necessary. Most organizations accept or request only the last four digits for identification. When a full SSN is required, use a secure transmission method (see below).

2) How to redact safely
- Work on a copy of the document — never overwrite the original scanned file.
- For scanned images (JPEG/PNG/PDF that is just an image):
  - Open in an image editor (Paint, Preview, Photos, Photoshop, GIMP).
  - Draw a solid black rectangle over the first five digits (or the full number leaving only the last 4 visible).
  - Save as a new file (PNG or flattened PDF). Image edits permanently remove the hidden text layer because the file is an image.
- For digital PDFs that may contain selectable text or hidden OCR layers:
  - Use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat “Redact” tool, or another trusted PDF redaction feature). The redaction tool actually removes the content, not just hides it.
  - After redaction, use “Sanitize” / “Remove hidden information” and Save As a new file.
  - If you don’t have a redaction tool, convert the page to an image (print to PDF or export as image), then redact the image as above and save — but confirm no searchable text remains.
- Avoid simply placing a black box annotation or shape over text in a PDF without flattening or removing the underlying text — those can sometimes be uncovered and the data retrieved.

3) Verify the SSN is gone
- Try to select/copy the area or search the saved file for the digits (6789, or the other digits if you remember them). If the full digits can be found by search or copy-paste, redaction was not complete.
- Open the file in a text editor to check for hidden text layers (for PDFs).
- Check file metadata and remove sensitive metadata with the PDF tool’s “remove hidden information” or “sanitize” option.

4) Secure transmission
- Prefer secure portals (employer/government portals, HTTPS uploads) over plain email.
- If you must email, use:
  - Encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) OR
  - Password-protected PDF (AES 256) and send the password by a different channel (SMS or phone).
- Ask the recipient if they have a secure upload option before sending.

5) Other safety steps
- Keep only the redacted copy you send; securely delete unredacted digital copies (empty recycle/trash, use secure-delete if available).
- Shred any physical unredacted printouts.
- Confirm in writing (or by phone) that the recipient only needs the last 4 if you don’t want to share the full SSN.

If you tell me which device and software you’re using (Windows, macOS, iPhone/Android, Adobe Acrobat, Preview, etc.), I can give step-by-step redaction instructions for that setup — and I’ll avoid repeating the full number you mentioned.

