Quick note: I noticed the full SSN you typed and the “last four” you gave don’t match — please double-check which one is correct before sending anything. I won’t repeat the numbers here.

Short answer
- Only include the minimum required information. If the requester only needs the last four digits, redact the rest.
- Never put an SSN in an email subject, file name, or unencrypted message body.
- Use a proper, permanent redaction method (not just drawing a black box in a viewer or covering with white digitally without removing underlying text) or send the full SSN only over a secure channel/portal.

Best-practice checklist for sending a scanned form that contains an SSN

1) Confirm what’s required
- Ask the recipient whether they accept just the last 4 digits for ID. Many organizations only need the last four.

2) Redact properly (permanent removal)
- Use a PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool is ideal): mark the characters to remove and Apply/Redact — this permanently eliminates the data and removes hidden text layers.
- If you don’t have Acrobat Pro, use a trustworthy PDF editor that supports permanent redaction (e.g., Nitro, PDF-XChange, or an official “redact” feature). Avoid simply adding a black rectangle in an editor that leaves the original text underneath.
- Option if you must work from paper: black out the SSN with an opaque permanent marker, then scan the result. After scanning, verify the text is gone (see verification step below).

3) Remove hidden data and metadata
- After redaction, “sanitize” or remove document metadata and hidden text layers (Acrobat has “Sanitize Document” / “Remove hidden information”; Word has “Inspect Document”).

4) Flatten the file
- Flatten or “print to PDF” (create a new PDF from the redacted file) so form fields or text layers can’t be extracted.

5) Verify redaction
- Try copying/searching the PDF for the redacted numbers. Open the file in a text editor or use PDF search to confirm the original digits aren’t present.
- If you scanned paper, zoom in on the redacted area to confirm no legible characters remain.

6) Secure transmission
- Use the recipient’s secure portal whenever possible.
- If you must email, send the PDF encrypted (S/MIME/PGP) or password-protect the PDF with a strong password and send the password separately (SMS or phone).
- Never send full SSN in plain-text email or unencrypted attachments. Don’t use public Wi‑Fi when sending sensitive files.

7) File naming and subject lines
- Don’t include SSN (full or last four) in the filename or the email subject. Use a neutral name like “Completed Form — [Your Name]”.

8) If you must send the full SSN
- Confirm why it’s needed, for how long it will be stored, and that the recipient has secure handling procedures.
- Prefer secure portals or in-person handoff.

If you’d like, tell me:
- Do you need to keep only the last 4 or must the recipient have the full SSN?
- What device/OS and software do you have (Windows, macOS, mobile, Adobe Acrobat, etc.)?

I can then give step‑by‑step redaction instructions for your setup or draft a short message you can send with the attachment (and a secure-password note).

