Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — if a scanned document contains your full Social Security number, you should avoid sending the full number unless the recipient explicitly requires it and provides a secure method. In most cases you can (and should) redact everything except the minimum requested (often just the last four digits).

Practical guidance

- Confirm what the requester actually needs
  - Ask whether they accept just the last four digits or a certified portal upload. Many organizations only need the last four.
  - If they insist on the full SSN, ask for a secure upload method (encrypted portal, secure link) and for their data handling policy.

- When redacting, make it permanent
  - Don’t simply hide text with a white box or blur — those can sometimes be reversed or remain in the file.
  - Use a true redaction tool that removes the underlying text/metadata (e.g., “Redact” in Adobe Acrobat Pro). If you don’t have a redaction tool, print the page, black marker the SSN, then scan the marked copy.

- Tools and methods (by common platform)
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Use the Redact tool -> Mark for Redaction -> Apply Redactions -> Save as a new file.
  - Mac Preview: Use the markup rectangle and fill with black, then export as PDF and test that text isn’t selectable/ searchable. (Safer: print and mark then re-scan.)
  - Windows: Use a PDF editor with redaction support, or print → mark with black ink → re-scan.
  - Mobile: Use a secure redaction feature in a trusted app or photograph the printed-and-marked copy. Avoid quick photo edits that only overlay pixels.
  - If using OCR or copying text, ensure the redacted PDF has no hidden text layers containing the SSN.

- Remove metadata and sensitive remnants
  - After redaction, save as a new file and remove metadata/EXIF (many PDF tools and image editors have metadata removal options).
  - Don’t include the SSN in the filename.

- Secure transmission
  - Prefer uploading to the requester’s secure portal (HTTPS) or a secure file-transfer service.
  - If you must email, send the file in a password-protected ZIP or PDF (strong password) and transmit the password separately (call or text).
  - Avoid sending full SSN over unencrypted email or messaging apps.

- Clean up after sending
  - Delete the temporary copies you created (scanned images, photos). Empty the device recycle/trash and remove backups if appropriate.
  - Confirm the recipient received only the redacted file and that they don’t need the original.

- When a full SSN is legitimately required
  - Confirm identity and legitimacy of the requester.
  - Use their secure upload method or deliver in person if possible.
  - Ask how they will store and destroy the document.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to the requester asking whether last four digits are sufficient and asking for a secure upload link, or
- Give step-by-step redaction instructions for your device (Windows, Mac, iPhone, Android), or
- Walk you through creating a password‑protected file and sending it securely.

Which would you like?

