Good question — you should be careful. A few high‑level rules, then practical steps.

High-level
- Never send your full SSN over an insecure channel (regular email, SMS, public cloud link) unless the recipient specifically requires it and provides a secure method.  
- Many forms only need the last four digits for identification; other forms (tax, payroll, government) often require the full SSN — always confirm what they actually need.  
- If you can, provide only the minimum (for example, only the last four) and use a secure submission method.

Practical checklist for sending a scanned document
1. Verify need and recipient
   - Ask the organization whether the full SSN is required, or if the last four digits will suffice. Request a secure upload portal or encrypted method if full SSN is required.

2. Redaction preferred method (digital)
   - Use a true redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool, PDF redaction software, or the organization’s portal) that permanently removes the underlying text, not just covers it visually.  
   - After redacting, save/export the PDF and confirm you cannot select or copy the redacted text. If the document was OCRed, re-run OCR or check text layer to ensure digits are gone.

3. If you must scan then redact
   - Scan the document to a PDF with good resolution.  
   - Redact digitally (black boxes) and then “flatten” or export to a new PDF so nothing remains editable underneath.  
   - Don’t rely on white rectangles or marker over printed text in a digital image unless you rescan the marked paper (and be sure the marker is opaque and permanent).

4. If you redact on paper first
   - Use a permanent black marker to obscure digits, then scan the paper. Verify the obscured digits can’t be seen through the ink or in the image. Better: redact digitally after scanning.

5. Secure delivery
   - Preferred: upload to the organization’s secure portal (SFTP, encrypted web form) or use encrypted email / password‑protected PDF.  
   - If you password‑protect the PDF, send the password by a separate channel (phone or SMS) — not in the same email.  
   - Avoid sending SSN via plain attachments in unencrypted email or via general file‑sharing links without password protection.

6. Remove extras and limit exposure
   - Remove any other unnecessary sensitive data (DOB, account numbers) unless required.  
   - Keep a record of when and to whom you sent the file, and delete leftover copies from your device and cloud storage once the transfer is complete (or archive securely).

7. Verify redaction
   - Try searching the PDF for your SSN digits; if nothing appears, redaction likely worked.  
   - Open the file on another device to confirm the digits are not visible or selectable.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short message asking the recipient whether the last four digits are sufficient and requesting a secure upload link; or
- Walk you step‑by‑step through redacting a PDF (tell me your OS and what software you have: Adobe, Preview on Mac, or Windows tools).

I won’t repeat your full SSN here — but it’s fine to confirm whether you want to share only the last four or need help getting a secure send method. Which would you like to do?

