2. Share only what's required
- If the form or recipient only needs the last four digits, redact the first five digits and send only the last four (e.g., XXX-XX-9012).
- If the recipient requires the full SSN, insist they provide a secure method for transmission (secure portal, encrypted upload) before you send the unredacted scan.

3. How to redact safely
- Digital PDFs: use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” feature or other PDF redaction that permanently removes content). Mark text to redact, Apply Redactions, then Save As a new file. Also “Sanitize” metadata if the tool offers it.
- Images / scans: do NOT just draw a black box as an annotation layer — that can be reversible. Instead either:
  a) Physically redact the paper with a permanent black marker, then make a photocopy of the redacted paper and scan the photocopy; or
  b) Open the scanned image in an image editor, paint over the digits (which alters pixels), flatten the image, then save as a new file (and confirm the digits are irrecoverable).
- Word / editable documents: convert to PDF and perform proper redaction on the PDF; do not leave the original editable file with the text intact.
- Avoid online redaction tools unless you trust the service and its privacy/security policies.

4. Remove metadata
- After redaction, remove or sanitize document metadata and EXIF data from images (PDF redaction tools often have a “sanitize” option; for images, use an EXIF remover).

5. Transfer securely
- Preferred: upload to the recipient’s secure portal or use encrypted file transfer (SFTP, secure upload link).
- If you must email: password-protect the PDF (AES-256 if available) and send the password by a different channel (text or phone call), not in the same email.
- Limit recipients and keep a copy only if necessary.

6. Keep records minimal
- Keep only what’s required and for as short a time as necessary. Treat SSNs as highly sensitive.

