High-level guidance
- Only share the full SSN when absolutely required. Often organizations only need the last 4 digits for ID; ask the recipient whether last-4 is sufficient.
- If you must send a scan containing an SSN, redact everything you don’t need. Prefer sharing only the last four digits (formatted like XXX-XX-6789) unless the form explicitly requires the full number.
- Use secure transmission: an encrypted upload portal, encrypted email (S/MIME/PGP/TLS + strong attached-file encryption), or a secure file-transfer service. Avoid sending full SSNs in plain email attachments.

How to redact safely (digital and physical)
- Best practice (digital): Use a PDF redaction tool that permanently removes the text and metadata (not just a black box). Examples: Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact tool or other reputable PDF editors that support proper redaction. After redaction, save a copy and verify the redacted area is not selectable or recoverable.
- If you only have basic tools: export the scan as an image, open it in an image editor, permanently erase the digits, then save a new PDF. Confirm there’s no hidden text layer or metadata containing the SSN.
- Physical document method: If you must redact a paper copy, cut out or fully and opaquely cover the SSN area (use an opaque label or thick permanent black label) before scanning. Simply putting a marker on top sometimes can be recovered by image processing if the ink isn’t opaque, so cutting out or using a trusted redaction sticker is safer.
