General guidance — what you should do when sending a scanned form that asks for an SSN

1. Only send the full SSN if it’s absolutely required
- Many organizations accept just the last four digits for identity verification. Ask the recipient whether last-four is sufficient before sending the full SSN.
- If the full SSN is required (e.g., some tax or government forms), use secure transfer methods (see below).

2. Prefer secure upload methods over email
- Use the organization’s encrypted portal, secure file upload, or a secure file transfer service. That’s the safest option.
- If they only accept email, ask for an encrypted email address or instructions for secure delivery.

3. Redaction best practices (digital, irreversible)
- Use a proper redaction tool (recommended):
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Save as new file.
  - Other PDF editors with a “redact” feature will similarly remove content from both the visible image and any underlying text/OCR layers.
- If you must edit an image:
  - Convert the scanned page to an image, apply a solid black box over the parts to remove, then flatten and re-save as a new PDF/image. Make sure to export/flatten so the hidden text/OCR is removed.
- Don’t “hide” by whiteing out text or cropping without removing underlying data — that can be recovered.
- After redaction, open the final file in another viewer and try selecting/copying text to verify the SSN cannot be recovered.

4. Mobile scanning apps
- Use apps that support permanent redaction or flattening (Adobe Scan, Microsoft Office Lens, iOS Notes markup then export as PDF).
- After redacting in-app, export and verify the SSN is unrecoverable.

5. Metadata and OCR
- Remove metadata (properties, author, comments) before sending.
- Ensure OCR text layer containing the SSN is removed or replaced by the redacted image.

6. Encryption and password protection
- If you must send via email, password-protect the PDF and share the password separately (e.g., text or phone call).
- Consider encrypting the email or using an encrypted attachment method.

7. Confirm and limit retention
- Tell the recipient you’ll delete your local copy (and ask them to confirm secure handling).
- Keep a record of what you sent (date, to whom) and delete copies from devices and cloud if not needed.

Quick checklist before sending a scanned form
- Confirm whether full SSN is required or last four will do.
- If full SSN required → use secure portal. If not, redact all but last four.
- Use a proper redaction tool; flatten file; strip metadata.
- Verify redaction by attempting to copy/select text.
- Send via secure channel or password-protect the file; share password separately.
- Ask recipient to confirm receipt and secure deletion if appropriate.

