Quick note before anything else: the full SSN you wrote ends with "9012" but you also listed the last four as "6789" — those don't match. Please confirm which is correct.

General guidance
- Only share a full SSN when absolutely required. Many forms only need the last four digits for verification.
- If only the last four are required, send only the last four (e.g., ****-**-9012 or simply 9012) — do not include the full SSN.
- Never put an SSN in an email subject line, filename, or unencrypted message body.

If you must send a scan that contains your full SSN
1. Ask for a secure method first
   - Request a secure upload portal, SFTP, or an encrypted email option (S/MIME or PGP). Ask the recipient if they can provide one.
2. If they insist on email, protect the file
   - Create a password‑protected PDF (use a strong password) and send the password separately (e.g., call or text).
3. Redact before scanning/sending (preferred)
   - Physical documents: permanently black out the SSN with a thick permanent marker, then photocopy or scan the redacted copy (not the original). Confirm the blacked area does not reveal text when held to light or copied.
   - Digital documents: use a proper PDF redaction tool (e.g., Adobe Acrobat’s Redact) that removes the underlying text — do NOT just place a black box over the text and save, because the underlying text can remain recoverable. After redaction, flatten and save as a new PDF and confirm the SSN cannot be selected or searched.
4. If you only need to show proof of identity, consider showing only the last four digits and blurring or redacting the rest.
5. Check metadata and hidden layers
   - Remove any metadata or embedded text layers before sending. Export to a flattened image/PDF if unsure.

Extra safety tips
- Verify the recipient’s identity and why they need the full SSN.
- Keep a record of when/why you shared it, and delete the transmitted file from unnecessary locations.
- If possible, ask whether an alternative ID (tax ID, passport, driver’s license) or last-four digits will suffice.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short message asking the recipient for a secure upload option? or
- Give step‑by‑step redaction instructions for Windows/Mac/phone or for Adobe Acrobat/Preview?

