What to do and why (practical steps)
1. Confirm whether the full SSN is actually required
   - Ask the requester whether they will accept just the last four digits on the scan, or whether a secure method is required for the full SSN.

2. Prefer secure channels
   - Use the organization’s secure upload portal or an encrypted file transfer. Do not send full SSNs over plain email or public messaging unless they specify and guarantee secure transmission.
   - If they require emailed documents, ask whether they accept a password‑protected PDF (and how to share the password).

3. Redact properly (if you’re sending a scan and full SSN is not required)
   - Permanently remove or obscure the digits you don’t want to share; leave only the last four visible.
   - Use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat’s Redact tool or other PDF redaction features) that removes underlying text and metadata.
   - If you don’t have a redaction tool: print the page, black it out with a permanent marker over unwanted digits, then scan the printed page. (Be sure the original file or an unredacted scan isn’t later sent by mistake.)
   - For images: paint over the digits and save as a new flattened image file (PNG/JPEG); verify there’s no selectable text or hidden layer.

4. Make the redaction unrecoverable
   - Avoid simply covering text with white boxes or annotation layers — those can sometimes be removed to reveal the original. Use tools that actually delete the underlying content or flatten the image.
   - After redaction, try selecting/searching text in the file — if you can still select the redacted digits, the redaction is not secure.

5. Remove metadata
   - Strip EXIF or document metadata before sending (many PDF editors have a “remove hidden information” or “remove metadata” option). You can also “Print to PDF” to flatten and remove some metadata, then check properties to confirm.

6. Protect the file in transit
   - Consider password‑protecting the PDF and share the password by phone or a separate channel.
   - Use end‑to‑end encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) only if both sides support it.

7. Keep records and double‑check recipient
   - Confirm the recipient’s exact email or upload link and that they are authorized to receive your SSN.
   - Retain only what you need; destroy or securely delete unneeded copies.

When full SSN must be sent
- If they legally require the full SSN, request their secure upload method. If they insist on email, ask for their encryption method or accept a password‑protected PDF.

