Short answer: yes — unless the recipient explicitly requires the full number, redact everything except the last four digits (you already gave those: 6789) before sending a scan. And when you must send the full SSN, use a secure method (secure upload portal, encrypted email, or hand-delivery).

Practical guidance and checklist

1) Confirm what the recipient actually needs
- Ask whether they accept “last four digits only.” Many forms and organizations only require the last four for identification.
- If they say they need the full SSN, ask for a secure submission method (secure portal, SFTP, encrypted e-mail) or arrange an in-person drop-off.

2) Redaction best practices (what to do)
- Permanently remove digits you don’t want to share; show only the last four (6789).
- Use a true redaction tool (that deletes content) rather than simply covering text with a black box on a layer that can be removed.
- Remove any hidden/embedded text and metadata (OCR text, EXIF, author fields).
- Send via a secure channel (secure upload portal is best). If using email, password-protect the file and share the password by a separate channel (phone or text).

3) How to redact safely (common methods)
- Adobe Acrobat Pro:
  - Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → mark the full SSN except the last four → Apply Redactions → Save (use “Save As” so the redaction is permanent).
  - Optionally password-protect the resulting PDF (File → Protect Using Password).
- Mac Preview (use with caution — some methods leave underlying text):
  - Convert the scan to an image (Export as PNG/JPEG), open in Preview, draw a filled black rectangle over the digits to remove, then File → Export as PDF. Confirm underlying text is gone by trying to select/copy text. If you need guaranteed redaction, use Acrobat or a dedicated redaction tool.
- Microsoft Word (if starting from an image):
  - Insert image, draw a filled black shape over unwanted digits, right-click shape → “Save as Picture” or Print to PDF to flatten. Double-check copy/paste.
- Image editor (Paint, Photos, Photoshop):
  - Paint over digits with solid color and save as a new image file (PNG/JPG). Converting to a flattened image guarantees removal of underlying text.
- Mobile phone:
  - Use a photo editor app to draw a solid black box and then save/export as a flattened image or PDF. Disable cloud backup before sending if concerned.

4) Remove metadata
- For images: strip EXIF data (many image editors or websites can do this).
- For PDFs: use your PDF tool’s “remove metadata” or check properties and clear fields.

5) Transmission security
- Best: upload via the organization’s secure portal.
- If email is the only option: password-protect the PDF and send the password in a separate channel (e.g., SMS or phone call). Use an encrypted email service if available.
- Avoid posting or sending over insecure public Wi‑Fi.

6) Verify after redaction
- Re-open the saved file and try to select/copy the redacted area to ensure the digits are not recoverable.
- Keep a record of who you sent it to and when.

7) If you mistakenly sent the full SSN
- Contact the recipient immediately and request secure deletion or confirm limited access.
- Monitor your credit and consider freezing your credit if you’re concerned.

