Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — unless the form specifically requires the full SSN, redact everything except the last four digits (the part most organizations accept). If the recipient actually needs the full SSN, send it only over a secure channel (secure upload portal, encrypted email, or password‑protected file shared separately).

Practical guidance

1) If you only need to show the last four
- Redact (hide or black out) the first five digits and the middle two digits so only the last four remain visible.
- Use a solid black bar (not whiteout or blur) so the underlying numbers cannot be recovered.

2) Redacting a paper document (recommended method)
- Make a copy of the original first and keep the original safe.
- On the copy, use a permanent black marker to cover the digits you want to hide. Let ink dry.
- Photocopy the redacted copy (the photocopy will show only the blacked‑out areas). Scan/convert that photocopy to PDF and send the photocopy scan — not the original or the pre‑marked scan.
- Alternatively, request the recipient’s secure upload instructions instead of emailing.

3) Redacting a digital file (PDF/image)
- Use a true redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact). Select text to redact, apply redactions, then save a new file. Acrobat’s Redact removes underlying text and metadata.
- If using an image editor, paint a solid black rectangle over the digits and save as a flattened image (JPEG/PNG) or print-to-PDF to ensure no hidden text layer remains.
- Do NOT rely on just “white covering,” highlighting, or visually obscuring text — these can sometimes be reversed or the underlying text remains in the file.
- If you used OCR when scanning, be sure to remove or avoid any hidden text layer that still contains the SSN.

4) Verify redaction
- Open the final file and try to select/copy the redacted text — you shouldn’t be able to copy the SSN.
- Search the PDF for the redacted numbers; they should not appear.
- Check file metadata and remove personal data (many PDF tools offer “Remove metadata”).

5) Secure sending
- Prefer recipient’s secure upload portal.
- If you must send by email: use encrypted email (S/MIME or TLS) or a password‑protected PDF (use a strong password and share the password over a different channel, e.g., phone).
- Avoid sending SSNs via plain email, SMS, chat apps, or unencrypted attachments.
- Confirm with the recipient exactly what they need (last 4 vs full SSN) and the secure method they prefer.

6) If the recipient requires the full SSN
- Ask if they have a secure upload portal or can accept a password‑protected file. If not, consider delivering in person or by a secure fax if available.
- Only send the full SSN when absolutely necessary and only via a secure method.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to the recipient asking whether they need the full SSN and what secure upload method they prefer.
- Walk you through redacting a specific PDF (tell me what tools you have: Adobe Acrobat, Preview, Windows, etc.).

