Short answer: yes — avoid sending the full SSN in an ordinary (unencrypted) email or an unprotected scan unless the recipient absolutely requires it and provides a secure way to receive it. Do not repeat the numbers you've given here; below I’ll give practical guidance without echoing them.

What to do before you send a scan
- Only include the last 4 digits when possible. Most organizations accept just the last four for identity confirmation.
- Ask the recipient if they really need the full SSN. If they do, request a secure upload portal or an alternative secure method (see options below).
- Verify the recipient and the purpose. Confirm it’s a legitimate request and that you’re sending to an official, expected address or portal.

If you must redact
- Best practice: permanently redact the SSN (black box/redaction) on the original paper before scanning, or use a proper PDF redaction tool that removes the underlying text. Do NOT just place a white box or crop in a way that leaves the original data recoverable.
- If you edit an image/photo, draw a solid black rectangle over the SSN and then save/export a new file. Confirm there is no unedited copy saved or embedded.
- After redaction, open the saved file and confirm you cannot select or reveal the redacted digits.

Secure ways to send an unredacted SSN (if required)
- Secure upload portal (recommended): official employer/agency portals are best.
- Encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP): only if you and recipient both support it.
- Password‑protected PDF: set a strong password, send the password by a different channel (text/call). Note: password-protected PDFs offer protection but are weaker than portal encryption.
- Encrypted archive (7‑zip with AES‑256) and share password separately.
- Fax to a verified number or send by secure courier/in person for highest assurance.

Other practical tips
- Remove metadata from images/PDFs (file properties can sometimes include identifying info).
- Don’t put SSNs in filenames or email subject lines.
- Use a different channel to share passwords or to confirm receipt.
- Keep a copy of what you sent and when, in case you need to verify later — but store it securely.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to request a secure upload link from the recipient.
- Give step‑by‑step redaction instructions for your device (Windows, macOS, iPhone/Android) or specific software (Adobe Acrobat, Preview, etc.).

