Quick note — the SSN you typed looks inconsistent (000-00-9012 vs. last four 6789). If you want advice tailored to that exact document, please confirm which is correct. Otherwise here are general, practical guidelines for redacting and sending scans safely.

When to redact
- If the recipient only needs the last 4 digits (common for identity checks), redact the rest and send only the last four.  
- If the recipient explicitly requires the full SSN (e.g., some payroll, tax, or government forms), do not redact — instead use a secure transmission method (see below). Always confirm with the requester exactly what they need before sending.

How to redact correctly (digital best practice)
1. Use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro, PDF Expert, Nitro, or your document editor’s official redact feature). Redaction permanently removes the underlying text/data, not just hides it visually.
2. After redacting, “apply” or “commit” the redaction so the original text is gone. Save as a new PDF.
3. Check by trying to select/search the redacted area — there should be no selectable/hidden text there.
4. Flatten the PDF / remove annotations and embedded metadata (Acrobat has “Remove hidden information” / “Sanitize document” tools).
5. Optionally password-protect the PDF (AES encryption) and share the password through a separate channel (phone or SMS).

If you only have a paper copy
- Best: use a black permanent marker to fully and completely black out digits, then scan the redacted document. After scanning, verify the redaction didn’t get recovered by enhancing contrast or OCR.
- Safer: use scissors to remove the SSN line before scanning, then scan the remainder of the document.
- Avoid: white-out or just covering with another piece of paper in the photo — these can sometimes be reversed.

If you’re taking a photo with a phone
- Cover the sensitive digits with a physical opaque sticker or paper before photographing, or use a reliable app that permanently blurs/redacts the area.
- Remove photo metadata (EXIF) before sending.

Secure transmission options
- Use the requester’s secure upload portal (preferred). Government, banks, employers typically provide these.
- If emailing: attach the redacted PDF, and either (a) send it as a password‑protected PDF and share the password separately, or (b) use encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) or your email provider’s secure message feature.
- Avoid uploading to unknown or untrusted third‑party websites.

Other tips
- Confirm exactly what the recipient needs (full SSN vs. last 4) before sending.
- Keep a copy of what you sent and delete any unneeded local copies after confirmation.
- Avoid sending SSN over public Wi‑Fi unless you are using a secure upload or encrypted connection.
- If you’re ever unsure, call the organization requesting the SSN and ask for their secure submission instructions.

If you want, I can:
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting a PDF on your device (tell me whether you’re on Windows, macOS, or mobile), or
- Draft a short message you can send to the requester asking whether the last four digits are sufficient and what secure upload method they prefer.

