Short answer: yes — avoid sending the full SSN in an unsecured scan. Sharing only the last four digits (which is commonly acceptable) is safer. If a form or recipient truly needs the full SSN, use a secure upload method or a purposely redacted and securely-transmitted file.

Recommended workflow (quick checklist)
1. Confirm requirement
   - Ask the recipient whether they need the full SSN or only the last four digits (you can tell them your last four are 6789).
2. Prefer secure channels
   - Use a secure upload portal, encrypted/form-only website, or an organization’s secure file transfer. Do NOT send full SSN in plain email or unsecured messaging.
3. If you must send a scan and only last four are required
   - Redact (remove) all other digits before scanning or before sending.
4. If you must send a full SSN
   - Only send via the recipient’s secure portal, encrypted email, password-protected PDF (share the password by phone or separate channel), or hand-delivery/in-person.
5. Permanently redact correctly (do not rely on simple overlays)
   - Physical method: black marker over the digits on the paper, then scan the marked page. (That burns the ink into the paper image.)
   - Digital method: use a true redaction tool that removes underlying content (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact tool, PDF editors with a “redact” feature). If you only draw a black box in a PDF overlay, the underlying text might still be recoverable — make sure to “apply” or “flatten” and then save a new file.
6. Remove metadata
   - After saving, strip metadata/EXIF (images) or PDF properties. Many apps let you “save as” a new file with no metadata; tools like exiftool can remove EXIF if you’re comfortable using them.
7. Verify the redaction
   - Open the final file and zoom/copy-paste to confirm the SSN digits are not recoverable. Check file properties for residual metadata.
8. Send safely
   - If you must email, attach the redacted file and also password-protect it; give the password over the phone or in a separate message.
9. Minimize copies
   - Keep as few copies as necessary, and securely dispose of/shred any paper copies with unredacted SSN once you’ve confirmed the recipient received what they need.
10. Ask recipient to confirm and delete
