Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — treat your full SSN as highly sensitive. Only include the full number when absolutely required, and never send it in an unencrypted email or an un-redacted scan. If the form only requires the last four (you said 6789), redact the other digits before scanning or sending.

Practical guidance and steps

1) Confirm what’s required
- Check the form instructions or ask the recipient whether they need the full SSN or if the last four (6789) will suffice. Only disclose the full SSN if they explicitly require it.

2) If only last four are required — redact the rest before scanning/sending
- Physical paper: permanently black out the first five digits with a thick permanent marker (not correction fluid). Better: cut out the part with the first five digits, then scan.
- Digital file: use a proper PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Sanitize Document). This removes the underlying data so it cannot be recovered.
- Do NOT just place a white rectangle or cover it in Word/Preview or an image editor without flattening/sanitizing — the text can remain in the file and be recovered.

3) If the full SSN is required — use a secure transmission method
- Preferred: upload via the recipient’s secure portal (most banks, government sites, employers provide one).
- If you must email, send a password-protected PDF encrypted with a strong password (AES-256). Share the password by a separate channel (phone call or SMS).
- Better options: end-to-end encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) or a secure file-sharing link with expiration and limited access (OneDrive/Box/Dropbox with link controls).
- Verify the recipient address and confirm their data-handling procedures.

4) Clean the file before sending
- Remove SSN from the filename.
- Sanitize metadata (in Acrobat: Sanitize Document or File → Properties → remove metadata).
- Flatten the PDF/image so there is no editable text layer.
- Double-check the saved file (open it and try to select/copy the redacted area — it should be gone).

5) Minimize exposure
- Only send one copy.
- Keep records of who received it.
- Delete local copies when no longer needed and empty the trash securely.

When not to send
- Don’t send full SSN in plain email, chat apps that aren’t end-to-end encrypted, or SMS (unless necessary and only for a password or code, not the full SSN).
- Don’t paste full SSN into files stored in shared folders without strict access controls.

If you want, I can:
- Help you draft a short message to the recipient asking whether the last four are sufficient or requesting a secure upload link.
- Walk you through redacting a PDF on your specific device (Windows/Mac/iPhone/Android) — tell me which one you use and which PDF app you have.

