User: I'm filling out a form that asks for my SSN. My SSN is 000-00-9012 (last four: 6789). Can you help me understand if I need to redact anything when sending a scan?
Assistant: Short answer: yes — redact any part of your Social Security number that is not required by the recipient, and avoid sending the full SSN over regular email unless absolutely necessary and the transfer is secure.

Important notes (I won't repeat the SSN you wrote):
- Only share the full SSN if the form or recipient explicitly requires it and they provide a secure way to submit it (secure portal, encrypted email, etc.).
- If the form asks only for the last four digits, redact (remove/black out) the first five digits before sending any scanned copy or image.

How to redact safely (two quick options)

1) Digital redaction (recommended if you can):
- Use a proper PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact; PDF Expert; or a reputable redaction feature in your PDF app). Mark the text to redact, Apply Redactions, then Save As a new file.
- After redaction, remove metadata and hidden text: check Document Properties and use any “Sanitize” or “Remove Hidden Information” feature.
- Verify by opening the final file and searching (Ctrl+F) for digits or the term “SSN” to make sure the redacted content is gone.
- If you must protect the file in transit, redact first, then optionally password-protect the PDF and send the password via a separate channel (SMS or phone call).

2) Physical redaction (good if you only have a paper copy):
- Make a photocopy of the original (do not redact the original).
- On the copy, use a permanent black marker to fully black out the parts you need to hide (cover the whole characters).
- Scan the redacted copy and inspect the scanned image to confirm nothing shows through.
- Discard the copy securely if needed.

Things to avoid
- Don’t just place a black box over the text in a Word document or image viewer and send the original PDF/Word — the underlying text or OCR layer may still be present and recoverable.
- Don’t include SSN digits in filenames, email subject lines, or the email body.
- Avoid sending full SSNs over plain, unsecured email unless the recipient requires it and uses a secure channel.

Secure transfer options (prefer these)
- Upload via the organization’s secure portal.
- Use encrypted/email or secure file transfer (SFTP, secure vendor portal).
- If you must email, send a redacted file and then send a password for the file by a different channel (phone/SMS).

Quick checklist before sending
- Is the full SSN required? If not → redact first five digits.
- Was a proper redaction tool used (not just a black box)?
- Metadata/hidden text removed?
- Filename and email body free of SSN?
- Are you using a secure transfer method or password-protecting and sharing the password separately?
- Confirm recipient’s secure handling policy and delete any local copies you don’t need.

If you want, I can:
- Walk you step-by-step through redaction on Windows or Mac,
- Draft a short secure-email message to the recipient,
- Or check whether the form actually requires the full SSN (you can paste the form text — but don’t paste the SSN itself).

Which would you like help with?

