Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: only share the full SSN if the recipient explicitly requires it for a legitimate purpose (tax forms, payroll, background check). Whenever possible, redact everything except the last four digits and send the full number only by a secure channel.

Guidance and practical steps

1) Before you send anything
- Ask the recipient what they actually need: full SSN, last four digits, or a redacted copy. Many organizations only need the last four.
- Confirm an approved secure method for transmission (secure portal, encrypted upload, or phone) rather than plain email.

2) If you can send only the last four
- Redact the rest so the full number cannot be reconstructed. Example: show only “•••-••-6789” (don’t leave faint/hidden text).
- Prefer a file format that preserves the redaction (PDF flattened, not an editable file).

3) If they require the full SSN
- Use a secure channel: employer/agency secure portal, bank/brokerage upload page, encrypted email, or give it by phone.
- If you must send a file: encrypt or password-protect the PDF and send the password separately (by phone or SMS). Avoid sending password and file together.

4) How to redact a scan so it’s permanent
- Best: use a proper PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” feature, or another PDF editor that supports redaction). Redaction tools remove the underlying text layer.
- If you only have image files: open in an image editor and draw an opaque black box over the digits, save as a new image, then convert to PDF. After conversion, check that text is not selectable (that would reveal underlying text).
- After redaction, “flatten” or “save as” a new file (no editable layers) and verify by trying to copy/paste the redacted area or opening in another viewer.
- Remove metadata (EXIF) from images/PDFs if possible (tools like exiftool or “remove properties and personal information” in Windows).

5) Extra precautions
- Keep a copy of what you sent and to whom, and note how it was sent.
- Don’t include SSNs in email subject lines or filenames.
- If you used a third-party service, confirm their data-handling policies and encryption.
- If you suspect the SSN was sent insecurely, contact the recipient and request confirmation of secure deletion or re-upload, and consider monitoring for identity fraud.

6) Quick message you can send to the recipient
“Hi — before I send my SSN, can you confirm whether you need the full SSN or only the last four digits, and which secure method you prefer for receiving it (secure portal, encrypted upload, or phone)? Thanks.”

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message tailored to the organization you’re dealing with.
- Walk you through redacting a specific file step-by-step (tell me whether you’re on Windows or Mac and whether you have Adobe Acrobat, Preview, or only scans/images).

