User: I'm filling out a form that asks for my SSN. My SSN is 000-00-9012 (last four: 6789). Can you help me understand if I need to redact anything when sending a scan?
Assistant: Short answer: only share the full SSN when it is absolutely required. In most cases you should send the minimum the recipient asks for — typically just the last four digits (e.g., XXX-XX-6789) — and redact the rest before scanning/ sending.

Practical guidance and steps

1) Confirm what they actually need
- Ask the recipient whether the last four digits are sufficient or whether they require the full SSN and why. If they require the full SSN, ask how they prefer you to transmit it (secure portal, encrypted email, etc.).

2) Redaction best practices (make removal permanent)
- Never just draw a black box in a PDF viewer that leaves the underlying text/OCR layer intact. That can be copied/recovered.
- Use a proper redaction tool or convert to an image so there is no hidden text layer.

Easy methods:
- Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions. This removes the underlying content permanently. Save a new file.
- Mac Preview: Export the page as an image (File → Export → Format: JPEG/PNG), open the image and black out digits, then save as a new PDF. (This eliminates hidden text.)
- Free fallback (Windows/Mac): Print the document, manually black out the SSN with a marker, then scan the redacted paper. (Physically blacking out + re-scan is reliable.)
- Image-editing: Open the scanned PDF in Paint/GIMP/Photoshop, block out the unwanted digits, then re-save as PDF.
- Online PDF editors: Only use reputable services and avoid uploading sensitive documents to unknown websites.

3) Remove metadata / OCR layer
- If your original was a searchable PDF, flatten or rasterize it so the redacted content can’t be recovered from the text layer.
- In Acrobat: File → Save As → Optimized PDF and remove hidden data, or use the “Sanitize Document” feature.
- Alternatively, convert to an image (PNG/JPEG) and re-create a PDF from that image.

4) Secure transmission
- Best: upload to the recipient’s secure portal (employer/payroll/IRS portals are preferable).
- If you must email:
  - Password-protect the PDF (AES-128/256). In Acrobat: File → Protect → Encrypt with Password.
  - Send the password by a separate channel (SMS or phone call), not in the same email.
  - If your organization uses encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP), use that.
- Avoid sending any SSN over public Wi-Fi without a VPN.

5) Keep minimal copies & verify retention
- Only keep a copy if you need it. If you must keep one, store it in an encrypted folder (e.g., FileVault on Mac, BitLocker on Windows, or an encrypted archive).
- Ask the recipient how long they will retain the SSN and how they protect it.

6) Quick checklist before sending
- Did you confirm whether full SSN is required? If not, ask.
- If only last four are needed, mask as XXX-XX-6789.
- Did you permanently redact the other digits (not just cover them visually)?
- Did you remove hidden text/OCR and metadata?
- Are you using a secure upload/email or a password-protected file with the password sent separately?

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the recipient asking whether last four digits suffice and requesting a secure upload method.
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting a document on your phone, Windows PC, or Mac — tell me which device and app you’re using.

