Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: only redact if the recipient does not actually need the full SSN. Many forms that ask for an SSN require the full number (they will reject a redacted version), but if they ask only for the "last 4" then you should send only those. Before sending anything, confirm with the organization which digits they require and which secure method they prefer.

Recommendations and safe procedures

- Confirm requirement first
  - Ask the recipient: do you need the full SSN, or will the last 4 digits suffice? Also ask how they prefer to receive it (secure upload portal, encrypted email, fax, etc.).

- If you only need to send the last four
  - Send only the last 4 digits (never include the rest). Example format: (last 4: 6789) — send just that.
  - Remove or redact the rest of the SSN on the document before sending.

- If you must send the full SSN
  - Use a secure channel: an encrypted upload portal, secure file transfer, or encrypted email. Avoid plain/unencrypted email whenever possible.
  - If you must attach a file, consider a password-protected PDF or an encrypted archive (e.g., ZIP/7z with AES-256) and send the password separately (by phone or different channel).

- How to redact properly (do NOT just place a white box over text)
  - For digital PDFs: use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool or an equivalent). Apply redactions and save — the underlying text is removed.
  - For Word documents: remove the SSN text, then export to PDF and sanitize/remove metadata.
  - For images/scans: either black out the number with a permanent marker on the original paper and then scan, or edit the image in an editor, draw a black box over the number, then export/save as a flattened image (PNG/JPEG). Verify the redacted area is not recoverable and that OCR text is not embedded.
  - For any file: “flatten” the document (save as a new image-based PDF) and remove document metadata before sending.
  - Avoid “hidden” redaction methods (white rectangles, layers, or simply hiding text) because those can be undone.

- Verify after redaction
  - Open the saved file on a different device to confirm the SSN is fully removed and not present in metadata or selectable text.
  - If possible, ask the recipient to confirm receipt and that the document meets their needs.

- Extra safety steps
  - Delete local copies you don’t need and securely shred any paper with full SSNs.
  - If you suspect any leakage, monitor credit reports and consider a fraud alert.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the recipient asking whether the full SSN is required and which secure method they prefer.
- Give step-by-step instructions for redacting a specific file type (PDF, Word, JPG) — tell me which you have.

