Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — unless the recipient specifically requires your full SSN, you should redact everything except the last four digits before sending a scanned document. If the recipient does need the full SSN, use a secure portal or encrypted transfer rather than an unprotected email/attachment.

Practical guidance and a safe workflow

1) Decide what’s required
- Check the form/recipient instructions. Many organizations only need the last four digits; some (tax or background checks) may require the full SSN and will provide a secure upload method.

2) Prefer secure transfer when full SSN is needed
- Use the service/portal the organization provides (secure upload, encrypted web form).
- If they insist on email, ask for an encrypted email option or agreement to receive it another way.

3) Redaction methods (digital — preferred if done correctly)
- Use a proper redaction tool so the removed text is permanently deleted (not just hidden).
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Sanitize Document → Save as a new file.
  - Other reputable PDF editors usually have a “redact” feature; confirm it permanently removes underlying text.
- If you don’t have a redaction tool:
  - Flatten the content after obscuring: place a solid black box over the SSN, then “print to PDF” or “export to PDF” to create a flattened file where the black box is embedded in the image.
  - Better: print the document, black out the number with a heavy permanent marker, then photocopy/scan the redacted paper and send the scan. (This physically removes the text.)

4) Avoid unsafe “redaction” approaches
- Don’t just change font color to white or draw a white box — underlying text or OCR layer can be recovered.
- Don’t rely on simple image overlay in an editor that keeps a text layer; always verify the layer is flattened.

5) Remove metadata and hidden content
- Use the PDF editor’s “Remove hidden information,” “Sanitize,” or “Document Inspector” features to remove metadata, attached files, comments, and OCR text layers.
- Check the PDF by searching for the SSN (or the first few digits) to make sure no recoverable text remains.

6) File name and other places to check
- Don’t include the SSN in the filename.
- Check all pages, headers/footers, embedded images, and thumbnails for the number.
- If the original scan was processed with OCR, ensure the redaction removes the OCR text.

7) Protect the file in transit
- Password-protect the PDF and share the password by a separate channel (phone, SMS).
- Or use secure file transfer (encrypted email, SFTP, secure portal, or a trusted file-sharing service with access controls).

8) Keep a record and confirm
- Note when and to whom you sent the file and ask the recipient to confirm receipt and that the document is acceptable.
- If requested, ask the recipient for their data retention policy and whether they will delete the document after use.

Quick checklist you can follow
- Confirm if full SSN is required. If not, redact everything but the last four.
- Use a true redaction tool or physical redaction + rescan.
- Sanitize the file (remove metadata and OCR text).
- Remove SSN from filename and all pages.
- Transfer via secure method or password-protect file.
- Confirm recipient received and doesn't need the full number.

If you want, tell me:
- Where you’re sending the scan (employer, government agency, bank, etc.), and whether they say they need the full SSN or only the last four — I can give a specific recommended method and step-by-step instructions for the software you have (Adobe, Preview on Mac, Windows tools, or scanning from paper).

