Short answer: Yes — unless the recipient specifically requires the full SSN for verification, you should redact everything except the last four digits before sending a scanned document. And if you must send the full SSN, use a secure submission method (secure portal, encrypted email, or password‑protected PDF) instead of plain email.

What to do — quick checklist
- Confirm requirement: Ask the recipient whether they need the full SSN or only the last four. If they only need the last four, redact the rest.
- Redact, don’t just hide: Use a true redaction tool so the removed numbers are irrecoverable (not just a black rectangle over selectable text).
- Use a secure channel: Upload to the organization’s secure portal, use encrypted email (S/MIME, PGP) or send a password‑protected PDF and deliver the password by a separate channel (phone/SMS).
- Minimize exposure: Only send to verified, trusted recipients and only the minimum information required.
- Sanitize the file: Remove metadata and OCR text that could contain the SSN (flatten the PDF or use a “remove hidden information” feature).
- Keep a secure copy: Keep an unredacted original only if necessary, stored securely.

How to redact and prepare a scanned file (common methods)
- Adobe Acrobat Pro:
  1. Use Tools > Redact. Select the numbers to redact; apply redactions.
  2. Use File > Save As to save a new file.
  3. Optionally: Tools > Protect > Encrypt with Password to password‑protect the file.
  4. Use “Remove Hidden Information” to clear metadata and hidden layers.

- macOS Preview:
  1. Use the Rectangle tool to draw over the SSN and fill with black, then Export as PDF.
  2. To reduce recoverability, Export > Print > Save as PDF (this flattens layers).
  3. To add password: File > Export as PDF > check “Encrypt”.

- Windows (if you don’t have Acrobat):
  1. Use a PDF editor with a redaction feature, or open the scanned image in Paint and permanently erase the numbers, save as new image/PDF.
  2. Print to “Microsoft Print to PDF” to flatten layers.
  3. Use a PDF password tool to encrypt if needed.

- Mobile scanner apps:
  - Use trusted apps that include redaction or permanent erase features (e.g., Adobe Scan + Acrobat mobile). After redaction, save and double‑check the PDF’s text layer isn’t searchable for the numbers.

Things people often miss
- Don’t just draw a black box over selectable text in a PDF viewer — the underlying text/OCR can still contain the SSN.
- Metadata and hidden layers may include the SSN — use a “sanitize” or “remove hidden data” feature or flatten the file.
- Don’t send the password for a protected file in the same email. Use a separate channel (call or SMS).
- Verify the recipient’s identity and use their official, secure upload link when available.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the requester asking whether the last four digits are sufficient or requesting a secure upload link.
- Give step‑by‑step redaction instructions for the specific device/software you have (tell me whether you’re on Windows, Mac, iPhone, or Android and which apps you have).

