Short answer: Only send whatever the requester actually needs. If the form (or recipient) only needs the last four digits, redact or remove the rest of the SSN before scanning/sending. If the form explicitly requires the full SSN, do not redact it — but send it only by a secure method (secure portal, encrypted email, password‑protected file), not as an unprotected email attachment.

Detailed guidance and practical steps

1) Decide what to include
- If the form asks only for "last 4 digits" or you can confirm the recipient only needs them, include just the last four.
- If the recipient explicitly requires the full SSN (e.g., tax, payroll, background check) only provide the full number by a secure channel they specify.

2) How to redact correctly (don’t just hide)
- Proper redaction removes the underlying text/data, not just visually covers it.
- Use a PDF redaction feature (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Redact tool → mark → Apply Redactions → Save / Sanitize). That removes the text layer and metadata.
- If you don’t have a redaction tool, convert the page to an image (print-to-PDF as raster or export to JPEG/PNG), then black out the digits with an editor, and save that image as a new PDF. This “flattens” the content so hidden text isn’t recoverable.
- Avoid simply placing a black rectangle annotation that can be removed or leaving searchable text under the box. Always verify the redaction.

3) Verify redaction
- After redacting, try to select or copy text in the PDF. If the SSN digits can be selected or searched, the redaction is not secure.
- Check document properties and remove metadata/hidden data (some PDF apps have “Sanitize” or “Remove hidden information”).

4) Physical redaction (paper)
- If you must redact on paper, use an opaque permanent marker to fully cover the digits, then scan that marked copy. Be aware that some markers can be read by strong light or image processing; digital redaction is safer.

5) Secure transmission
- Use the sender’s secure upload portal if available (preferred).
- If sending by email, password-protect/encrypt the PDF and share the password via a different channel (text/call).
- Do not put SSNs in the email subject or file name.
- Consider S/MIME or PGP encrypted email if you use it.

6) Minimal exposure & audit
- Only include the minimum information required.
- Keep a copy of where/how you sent it and to whom (for your records).
- If unsure, ask the recipient: “Do you need the full SSN, or will the last four suffice? If you need the full SSN, what secure method should I use to submit it?”

If you want, tell me:
- whether the form actually requires the full SSN or only the last four, and
- which device/tool you’ll use to scan/send (Windows, Mac, phone, Adobe, Preview, etc.),

and I’ll give step‑by‑step instructions for secure redaction and sending.

